tio. The total length is 360 feet, and the remoras are 6*72 inches. But since the units are different, converting is necessary. So yeah, converting to inches is the way to go. \n\nAlternatively, if I convert the total length to remoras' units. But that might complicate. Let's see. 360 feet to inches is 4320 inches. Each remora is 72 inches. So how many remoras are in the total length? 4320 /72. Let's compute that. 4320 divided by 72. 72*60=4320. So 60 remoras. But Emma only saw 6 remoras. So 6/60=0.1=10%. That's another way to see it. So 6 out of 60 remoras, which is 10%. \n\nSo all methods lead to 10%. So I think that's correct. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe combined length of the remoras is \\boxed{10\\%} of the dolphin's body length.\n</think>\n\nEmma saw a 360-foot dolphin with 6 remoras, each 72 inches long. To find the percentage of the dolphin's body length that is the combined length of the remoras, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the dolphin's body length from feet to inches:\n   \\[\n   360 \\text{ feet} \\times 12 \\text{ inches/foot} = 4320 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the total length of the remoras:\n   \\[\n   6 \\text{ remoras} \\times 72 \\text{ inches/remora} = 432 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. Determine the percentage of the dolphin's body length that is the combined length of the remoras:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{432 \\text{ inches}}{4320 \\text{ inches}} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{1}{10} \\right) \\times 100 = 10\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the combined length of the remoras is \\boxed{10\\%} of the dolphin's body length.", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 10.0, "is_correct": true}
