fog bank is moving at a certain speed, then the time to cover the entire span would depend on how much of the span is being covered per unit time.\n\nWait, but if the fog bank is moving from the ocean towards the city, then the front of the fog is moving towards the city. So, the distance that needs to be covered is 63 miles, and the front of the fog is moving at a certain speed. So, the time would be 63 divided by the speed.\n\nBut the problem states that it takes 30 minutes to cover every 21 miles of the city. So, perhaps the entire 21-mile stretch is covered in 30 minutes. So, the speed is 21 miles per 30 minutes. Then, the total distance is 63 miles, so time is 63 / (21 / 30) = 63 * (30 / 21) = 63 * (10 / 7) = 90 minutes. So, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, if the fog bank is moving such that it covers 21 miles in 30 minutes, then the rate is 21 miles per 30 minutes. So, in terms of the entire city, which is 63 miles, the time would be (63 / 21) * 30 minutes. Which is 3 * 30 = 90 minutes. So, same result.\n\nTherefore, regardless of how I approach it, the answer seems to be 90 minutes.\n\nBut let me check again. Let's think of it as the fog bank is moving at a certain speed. Let's say the speed is v miles per minute. Then, in 30 minutes, it covers 21 miles. So, v = 21 / 30 miles per minute. Then, the total time to cover 63 miles is t = 63 / v = 63 / (21 / 30) = 63 * (30 / 21) = 90 minutes. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is trying to trick me into thinking that the fog is moving from the ocean to the city, so the distance the fog has to cover is 63 miles, but the speed is 21 miles per 30 minutes. So, time is 63 / (21 / 30) = 90 minutes. So, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is presented as the fog bank is moving across the city, so the front of the fog is moving towards the center of the city. So, the distance from the front to the edge of the city is 63 miles. Wait, but the city is 63 miles across from oceanfront to inland edge. So, the total distance the fog has to cover is 63 miles. If the front of the fog is moving towards the center, then the time would be 63 divided by the speed.\n\nBut the problem says it takes 30 minutes to cover every 21 miles of the city. So, perhaps each 21-mile stretch is covered in 30 minutes. So, the front of the fog moves 21 miles in 30 minutes. Therefore, the speed is 21 miles per 30 minutes. Then, the total distance is 63 miles, so time is 63 / (21 / 30) = 90 minutes. So, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the fog bank is moving from the ocean to the city, so the entire 63-mile span is moving towards the city. Wait, but that would mean the front of the fog is moving towards the city, and the rest of the fog is following. So, the distance that needs to be covered is 63 miles, and the speed is 21 miles per 30 minutes. Therefore, time is 63 / (21 / 30) = 90 minutes. So, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the fog bank is moving at a certain rate, and the 21 miles is the distance it covers while the city is moving? Wait, but the problem doesn't mention the city moving. It just says the fog bank is rolling in from the ocean to cover the city. So, the city is stationary, and the fog is moving towards it. So, the distance the fog needs to cover is 63 miles, and the rate is 21 miles per 30 minutes. Therefore, time is 63 divided by (21 / 30) = 90 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the fog bank is moving such that it covers 21 miles in 30 minutes, but the city is 63 miles in total. So, how many 21-mile segments are there in 63 miles? There are 3. So, 3 times 30 minutes is 90 minutes. So, same answer.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the approach, the answer seems to be 90 minutes. So, I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{90} minutes.\n</think>\n\nThe problem states that a fog bank takes 3", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 90.0, "is_corr