{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 95, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 9 minutes to cover every 3 miles of the city. If the city is 18 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 9 minutes to cover every 3 miles of the city. If the city is 18 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about a fog bank rolling into a city. It takes 9 minutes to cover every 3 miles of the city. The city is 18 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge. We need to find out how many minutes it will take for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n\nHmm, first, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. The fog bank moves from the ocean towards the city, covering 3 miles in 9 minutes. The city's width is 18 miles. So, the question is, how long does it take for the entire 18-mile span to be covered by the fog?\n\nWait, but the problem says \"cover every 3 miles of the city.\" So, does that mean that the fog bank moves 3 miles in 9 minutes? Or is it that the entire 18-mile city is covered, which would require moving 18 miles? But the time given is for 3 miles. Hmm, maybe I need to think about the rate at which the fog is moving.\n\nLet me break it down. If the fog bank covers 3 miles in 9 minutes, then its speed is 3 miles per 9 minutes. To find the time it takes to cover 18 miles, I can use the formula: time = distance / speed.\n\nFirst, calculate the speed. 3 miles in 9 minutes. So, speed = 3/9 miles per minute. Simplifying that, 3 divided by 9 is 1/3. So, the speed is 1/3 miles per minute.\n\nNow, the total distance to cover