 of cooking also pop from the residual heat.\" So, after the pan is turned off, those 100 pops from the final 14 seconds, a quarter of them (25) start popping again due to residual heat. So, those 25 would be added to the total.\n\nTherefore, total popcorns would be 40 + 160 + 200 + 100 + 25 = 525.\n\nWait, but let me check if the residual heat is considered as separate from the popping during the intervals. The problem says \"after Thiago takes the pan off the heat, a quarter of the number of kernels that popped in the final 14 seconds of cooking also pop from the residual heat.\" So, the residual heat causes some of the popped kernels to pop again. But does that mean that the total number of pops is the sum of the pops during each interval plus the residual pops? Or is the residual heat causing additional popping during some time after the pan is turned off?\n\nThe problem says \"after the pan is turned off, a quarter... pop from the residual heat.\" So, the residual heat is causing some of the popped kernels to pop again. But how much time does that take? The problem doesn't specify, so maybe we can assume that the residual heat causes those 25 to pop immediately after the pan is turned off, so adding 25 to the total.\n\nTherefore, the total number is 525. Hmm, but let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe the residual heat is causing popping during the final 14 seconds? Wait, no. The final 14 seconds is when the rate slows down to 100 per 14 seconds. Then, after the pan is turned off, a quarter of the final 14 seconds' pops (which were 100) pop from residual heat. So, the residual heat is causing 25 more pops. But does that 25 happen during the final 14 seconds or after?\n\nThe problem says \"after Thiago takes the pan off the heat, a quarter... pop from the residual heat.\" So, after the pan is turned off, meaning after the final 14 seconds. So, the 25 pops would occur after the pan is turned off, not during the final 14 seconds. Therefore, we just add 25 to the total