{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 332, "question": "Ibrahim has 65 square yards of watermelon field. There are 85 watermelons per square yard. Ibrahim can harvest his watermelons every 3 months. How many watermelons can Ibrahim harvest within a year?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Ibrahim has 65 square yards of watermelon field. There are 85 watermelons per square yard. Ibrahim can harvest his watermelons every 3 months. How many watermelons can Ibrahim harvest within a year?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Ibrahim has 65 square yards of watermelon field. Each square yard produces 85 watermelons. He can harvest every 3 months. We need to find out how many watermelons he can harvest in a year. Hmm, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out how many watermelons one square yard can produce in a year. Since each square yard has 85 watermelons per harvest, and he harvests every 3 months, I should calculate how many times he can harvest in a year. A year has 12 months, right? So dividing 12 by 3 gives 4. That means he can harvest 4 times a year. \n\nSo, for one square yard, the total watermelons would be 85 watermelons per harvest multiplied by 4 harvests. Let me write that out: 85 x 4. Let me do the multiplication. 80 x 4 is 320, and 5 x 4 is 20, so 320 + 20 = 340. So each square yard produces 340 watermelons in a year.\n\nBut Ibrahim has 65 square yards. So we need to multiply the number of watermelons per square yard by the number of square yards. That would be 340 x 65. Hmm, that's a bigger number. Let me calculate that. \n\nBreaking it down, 340 x 60 is 20,400, and 340 x 5 is 1,700. Adding those together: 20,400 + 1,700 = 22,100. So, 340 x 65 = 22,100. Therefore, Ibrahim can harvest 22,100 watermelons in a year.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. First, 85 watermelons per square yard every 3 months. 12 months divided by 3 is indeed 4. So 85 x 4 is 340. Then 340 per square yard times 65 square yards. Let me verify 340 x 65 again. \n\nAlternatively, 65 can be broken into 60 + 5. So 340 x 60 = 20,400 and 340 x 5 = 1,700. Adding those gives 20,400 + 1,700 = 22,100. Yep, that seems right. \n\nAnother way to check: 65 x 340. Let's do 65 x 300 = 19,500 and 65 x 40 = 2,600. Then 19,500 + 2,600 = 22,100. Same result. Okay, that seems consistent. \n\nSo, each square yard gives 340 watermelons a year, and with 65 square yards, it's 22,100 total. Therefore, Ibrahim can harvest 22,100 watermelons within a year. \n\nI think that's all. Let me just make sure there's no step I missed. The problem says he can harvest every 3 months, so 4 times a year. Each harvest is 85 watermelons per square yard. Multiply those together for each square yard, then multiply by 65. Yep, that's covered. So the answer should be 22,100 watermelons.\n</think>\n\nIbrahim can harvest watermelons 4 times a year (since 12 months / 3 months = 4 harvests). Each square yard produces 85 watermelons per harvest, so per year:  \n\\[ 85 \\, \\text{watermelons/harvest} \\times 4 \\, \\text{harvests/year} = 340 \\, \\text{watermelons/year per square yard} \\]  \nFor 65 square yards:  \n\\[ 340 \\, \\text{watermelons/year/square yard} \\times 65 \\, \\text{square yards} = 22,100 \\, \\text{watermelons/year} \\]  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{22100}", "model_answer": 22100.0, "ground_truth": 22100.0, "is_correct": true}
