hs:\n\n1. January\n2. February\n3. March\n4. April\n5. May\n6. June\n7. July\n8. August\n9. September\n10. October\n11. November\n12. December\n\nIf he starts in January, then the next harvest would be May (4 months later), then September, then January of the next year. So, within a single year, he can do three harvests: January, May, September. The next would be March of the next year, which is outside the current year. Therefore, three harvests in a year. Therefore, each harvest is 7,735 apricots. So, 3 * 7,735 = 23,205.\n\nAlternatively, if he can harvest every 4 months regardless of the month, meaning that he can harvest in between months, but that seems unlikely. The problem probably means that he can harvest 4 times a year, each 4 months apart. Therefore, three times. So, 23,205.\n\nBut wait, the other approach: total apricots per hectare per year is 91 * 4 = 364. Then, 85 *", "model_answer": 85.0, "ground_truth": 23205.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 334, "question": "Jamal has 25 hectares of pomegranate field. There are 36 pomegranates per hectare. Jamal can harvest his pomegranates every 4 months. How many pomegranates can Jamal harvest within a year?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Jamal has 25 hectares of pomegranate field. There are 36 pomegranates per hectare. Jamal can harvest his pomegranates every 4 months. How many pomegranates can Jamal harvest within a year?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Jamal has 25 hectares of pomegranate field. Each hectare has 36 pomegranates. He can harvest his pomegranates every 4 months. The question is, how many pomegranates can he harvest within a year?\n\nFirst, I need to figure out the total number of pomegranates he has. Since there are 25 hectares and each hectare has 36 pomegranates, I should multiply 25 by 36 to get the total number of pomegranates. Let me do that: 25 times 36. Hmm, 25 times 30 is 750, and 25 times 6 is 150, so adding those together gives 750 + 150 = 900. So, there are 900 pomegranates in total.\n\nNow, he can harvest every 4 months. A year has 12 months, so how many 4-month periods are there in a year? Let me divide 12 by 4. 12 divided by 4 is 3. So, there are 3 harvest periods in a year.\n\nEach time he harvests, he gets 900 pomegranates. So, if he harvests three times, that's 900 multiplied by 3. Let me calculate that: 900 times 3. 900 times 2 is 1800, plus another 900 is 2700. So, 2700 pomegranates in total.\n\nWait, let me double-check. 25 hectares times 36 pomegranates per hectare is indeed 900. Then, since he harvests every 4 months, and there are 12 months, dividing 12 by 4 gives 3. Multiply 900 by 3 gives 2700. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by calculating the total harvest per month and then multiply by 12. Let's see. If he can harvest 900 pomegranates every 4 months, then per month it would be 900 divided by 4. 900 divided by 4 is 225. So, 225 pomegranates per month. Then, 225 times 12 months would be 225*12. Let's compute that. 200*12=2400, 25*12=300, so 2400+300=2700. Yep, same answer. So that confirms it.\n\nTherefore, Jamal can harvest 2700 pomegranates within a year.\n</think>\n\nJamal has 25 hectares of pomegranate field, with 36 pomegranates per hectare. The total number of pomegranates is calculated as:\n\\[\n25 \\text{ hectares} \\times 36 \\text{ pomegranates/hectare} = 900 \\text{ pomegranates}\n\\]\nSince he harvests every 4 months, the number of harvest periods in a year is:\n\\[\n\\frac{12 \\text{ months}}{4 \\text{ months/harvest}} = 3 \\text{ harvest periods}\n\\]\nMultiplying the total pomegranates by the number of harvest periods gives:\n\\[\n900 \\text{ pomegranates/harvest period} \\times 3 \\text{ harvest periods} = 2700 \\text{ pomegranates}\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{2700}", "model_answer": 2700.0, "ground_truth": 2700.0, "is_correct": true}
