ips does the farmer need to transport all the feed?\" So, if the total is exactly 14*130, then 14 trips. But let me confirm once more.\n\nTotal kg: 1820. 130*14=1820. So yes, 14 trips. So the answer is 14.\n\nBut let me check once again if I added all the quantities correctly. Maybe I made a mistake in the addition.\n\nStarting over:\n\nHay: 28 kg\n\nCarrots: 126 kg\n\nWheats: 1360 kg\n\nOats: 306 kg\n\nTotal: 28 + 126 = 154\n\n154 + 1360 = 1514\n\n1514 + 306 = 1820. Correct.\n\n1820 divided by 130. 130*10=1300, 1820-1300=520. 520/130=4. So total 10+4=14. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the farmer needs 14 trips.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Let me check if the problem mentions anything about the truck's capacity. The problem says \"the farm truck can carry 130 kgs at a time\". So each trip can carry up to 130 kg. So total 1820 kg divided by 130 kg per trip is 14 trips. So 14 trips. So the answer is 14.\n\nBut let me check once again if I calculated each type correctly.\n\nHay: 7 boxes *4 kg=28. Correct.\n\nCarrots:14*9=126. Correct.\n\nWheats:34*40=1360. Correct.\n\nOats:18*17=306. Correct.\n\nTotal:28+126=154; 154+1360=1514; 1514+306=1820. Correct.\n\n1820 divided by 130. 130*14=1820. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 14 trips.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe farmer needs \\boxed{14} trips to transport all the feed.\n</think>\n\nThe farmer is buying four types of feed: hay, carrots, wheats, and oats. We need to calculate the total amount of each type and then determine the number of trips required for the truck to transport all the feed, given a capacity of 130 kg per trip.\n\n1. **Hay**: 7 boxes of 4 kg each.\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 4 = 28 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Carrots**: 14 bags of 9 kg each.\n   \\[\n   14 \\times 9 = 126 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Wheats**: 34 packs of 40 kg each.\n   \\[\n   34 \\times 40 = 1360 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Oats**: 18 sacks of 17 kg each.\n   \\[\n   18 \\times 17 = 306 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\nAdding all these quantities together:\n\\[\n28 + 