nds.\n2. **Carrots**: 11 bags, each 7 pounds.\n3. **Corns**: 32 packs, each 44 pounds.\n4. **Apples**: 2 sacks, each 16 pounds.\n\nWait, hold on. The problem says he buys seven 5-pound boxes of wheats. So that's 7 boxes × 5 pounds each. Then eleven 7-pound bags of carrots. So 11 × 7. Then thirty-two 44-pound packs. 32 × 44. And twenty 16-pound sacks. 20 × 16. Then sum all those up.\n\nLet me compute each one step by step.\n\nStarting with wheats: 7 boxes × 5 pounds. 7 × 5 is 35 pounds.\n\nCarrots: 11 bags × 7 pounds. 11 × 7. Let's calculate that. 10×7=70, plus 1×7=7, so 70+7=77 pounds.\n\nCorns: 32 packs × 44 pounds. Hmm, 32×44. Let me break that down. 30×44=1320, and 2×44=88. So 1320+88=1408 pounds.\n\nApples: 2 sacks ×16 pounds. 2×16=32 pounds.\n\nNow, summing all these up:\n\n35 (wheat) + 77 (carrots) + 1408 (corns) + 32 (apples).\n\nLet's add them step by step.\n\nFirst, 35 + 77. 35 + 70 is 105, plus 7 is 112.\n\nThen, 112 + 1408. 112 + 1400 is 1512, plus 8 is 1520.\n\nNext, 1520 + 32. 1520 + 30 is 1550, plus 2 is 1552.\n\nSo total weight is 1552 pounds.\n\nNow, the truck can carry 230 pounds per trip. So we need to divide 1552 by 230 to find the number of trips.\n\nLet me compute 1552 ÷ 230.\n\nFirst, see how many times 230 fits into 1552.\n\n230 × 6 = 1380.\n\n230 × 7 = 1380 + 230 = 1610.\n\nBut 1610 is more than 1552, so it's between 6 and 7 trips.\n\nCompute 1552 - 1380 = 172. So 6 trips would carry 1380 pounds, and then there's 172 pounds left. Since each trip can carry 230 pounds, even though 172 is less than 230, he needs another trip. So total trips would be 7.\n\nWait, but let me check the division again. 230 × 6.5? Wait, no. Wait, 230 × 6 = 1380. 230 × 7 = 1610. 1552 is between those. So 1552 ÷ 230 = 6.752... So approximately 6.752 trips. Since you can't do a fraction of a trip, you have to round up to 7 trips.\n\nBut wait, let me verify the total weight again. Maybe I made a mistake in adding.\n\nStarting over:\n\nWheats: 7×5=35\n\nCarrots:11×7=77\n\nCorns:32×44. Let's compute 32×40=1280, 32×4=128, so 1280+128=1408\n\nApples:2×16=32\n\nTotal:35+77=112; 112+1408=1520; 1520+32=1552. Yes, that's correct.\n\n1552 divided by 230. Let's do this division properly.\n\n230 × 6 = 1380\n\n1552 - 1380 = 172\n\nSo 172 left. 172 / 230 is approximately 0.752. So total trips 6 + 0.752 ≈ 6.752. So 7 trips needed.\n\nBut wait, maybe there's a different way to compute. Let me check if 230 × 7 is 1610, which is more than 1552. So yes, 7 trips.\n\nBut hold on, maybe the problem expects us to consider that the truck can carry up to 230 pounds, so even if the last trip isn't full, it still counts as a trip. So even if he has 172 pounds left, he needs another trip. So total trips 7.\n\nBut let me check once more. Maybe I miscalculated the total weight.\n\nWheats: 7×5=35\n\nCarrots:11×7=77. 11×7=77, correct.\n\nCorns:32×44. Let's compute 32×44. 32×40=1280, 32×4=128. 1280+128=1408. Correct.\n\nApples:2×16=32. Correct.\n\nTotal:35+77=112; 112+1408=1520; 1520+32=1552. Correct.\n\n1552 divided by 230. Let's do 230×6=1380. 1552-1380=172. 172/230=0.752. So 6.752 trips. Since he can't do a partial trip, he needs 7 trips.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 7 trips.\n\nWait, but let me check once again. Maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication.\n\nWait, 32×44. Let's compute 44×30=1320, 44×2=88. 1320+88=1408. Correct.\n\n11×7=77. Correct.\n\n7×5=35. Correct.\n\n2×16=32. Correct.\n\nTotal:35+77=112. 112+1408=1520. 1520+32=1552. Correct.\n\n1552 divided by 230. Let me do this division step by step.\n\n230 × 6 = 1380. 1552 - 1380 = 172.\n\n172 divided by 230. So 172/230 = 0.752. So total trips 6.752. Since he can't do a fraction, he needs 7 trips.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 7.\n\nBut wait, let me check if the problem says \"at a time\". So each trip is 230 pounds. So even if the last trip is only 172 pounds, he still has to make that trip. So total trips 7.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem expects us to use integer division. 1552 ÷ 230 = 6.752, which would round up to 7. So yes, 7 trips.\n\nTherefore, the farmer needs 7 trips.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe farmer needs \\boxed{7} trips to transport all the feed.\n</think>\n\nThe farmer is buying different feeds for his sheep and needs to determine how many trips his truck can carry. The truck can carry 230 pounds per trip. The total weight of each type of feed is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Wheats**: 7 boxes × 5 pounds = 35 pounds\n2. **Carrots**: 11 bags", "model_answer": 11.0, "ground_truth": 8.0, "is_correct": false}
