en plus 8 is 291. So, the difference is 18. Therefore, 18 olives won't be able to fit on the plates.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. So, 21 times 13. Let's do it another way. 13 times 20 is 260, and 13 times 1 is 13. So, 260 plus 13 is 273. Yeah, that's correct. Then 291 minus 273. Let's subtract step by step. 291 minus 200 is 91. Then subtract 73 more. 91 minus 70 is 21, then minus 3 is 18. Yep, that's 18. So, the answer should be 18.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as division. If he has 291 olives and 13 plates, each holding 21, how many can he place per plate? So, 291 divided by 13. Let me compute that. 13 times 20 is 260. 291 minus 260 is 31. Then, 13 goes into 31 twice, which is 26. 31 minus 26 is 5. So, that's 20 plus 2 is 22, with a remainder of 5. So, 22 times 13 is 286, and 291 minus 286 is 5. Wait, that contradicts my previous answer. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, hold on. There's a mistake here. If each plate can hold 21 olives, then dividing 291 by 13 gives how many plates he needs to use. But if he can only use whole plates, then the quotient would be the number of plates used, and the remainder would be the number of olives left. But in this case, the remainder is 5. But earlier, when I multiplied 21 by 13, I got 273, and 291 minus 273 is 18. So, which is it?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me clarify. If he wants to place all 291 olives on the plates, each holding 21, how many plates does he need? So, 291 divided by 21. Let's compute that. 21 times 10 is 210. 291 minus 210 is 81. 21 times 4 is 84. So, 81 is 21 times 3.5... Wait, no. Wait, 21 times 3 is 63. 81 minus 63 is 18. So, 10 plus 3 is 13, and 18 left over. So, 291 divided by 21 is 13 with a remainder of 18. Wait, that's different from the previous division. Wait, so which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the division steps. Let me try again. 21 times 10 is 210. 291 minus 210 is 81. Then, 21 times 3 is 63. 81 minus 63 is 18. So, total is 10 + 3 = 13, remainder 18. So, 291 divided by 21 is 13 with a remainder of 18. Therefore, he needs 13 plates, which he has, and he can place 21*13=273 olives, leaving 18 left. Therefore, the answer is 18. So, why did I get confused earlier?\n\nBecause when I did 291 divided by 13, I thought of it as how many plates he needs, but actually, each plate can hold 21, so the number of plates required is 291 divided by 21, which is 13.5. But since he can't use half a plate, he needs 14 plates. But he only has 13. Therefore, he can't fill the last plate. So, the remainder is 18. So, that's the same as before. So, the answer is 18. So, the confusion was because I misapplied the division. So, the correct answer is 18.\n\nAlternatively, if he uses 13 plates, each with 21 olives, that's 273. 291 minus 273 is 18. So, 18 can't be placed. Therefore, the answer is 18. So, that's consistent. So, the answer is 18. Therefore, the number of olives he won't be able to place is 18.\n\nBut let me just verify once more. Let's compute 21*13. 20*13=260, 1*13=13, so 260+13=273. Then 291-273=18. Yes. So, that's correct. So, the answer is 18. So, I think that's solid. So, the answer is 18.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of olives that won't fit is \\boxed{18}.\n</think>\n\n Oliver has 291 olives and 13 plates, each holding 21 olives. To find out how many olives he won't be able to place, we first calculate the maximum number of olives that can be placed on the plates:\n\n\\[\n21 \\text{ olives per plate} \\times 13 \\text{ plates} = 273 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the number of olives that can be placed from the total number of olives he has:\n\n\\[\n291 \\text{ total olives} - 273 \\text{ olives placed} = 18 \\text{ olives left}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of olives that won't be able to fit on the plates is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).", "model_answer": 18.0, "ground_truth": 18.0, "is_correct": true}
