subtraction here. So the answer should be 124.\n</think>\n\nJin can place \\(24 \\text{ crackers/tray} \\times 8 \\text{ trays} = 192 \\text{ crackers}\\) on the trays. Subtracting this from the total number of crackers gives \\(316 - 192 = 124\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{124}\\)", "model_answer": 124.0, "ground_truth": 124.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 604, "question": "Ravi places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 37 almonds. If he has 328 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Ravi places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 37 almonds. If he has 328 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Ravi has 328 almonds and 7 tables. Each table can hold 37 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of almonds he can place on the 7 tables and then subtract that from the total number of almonds he has. The difference should be the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the 7 tables. Since each table holds 37 almonds, for 7 tables it would be 37 multiplied by 7. Let me do that multiplication. 37 times 7... Let's break it down: 30 times 7 is 210, and 7 times 7 is 49. Adding those together, 210 + 49 equals 259. So the tables can hold a total of 259 almonds. \n\nNow, Ravi has 328 almonds. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of almonds he has. So that's 328 minus 259. Let me compute that. 328 minus 200 is 128, then minus 59 more. 128 - 50 is 78, and then minus 9 is 69. So 328 - 259 equals 69. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 37 times 7: 37*7. 7*7 is 49, 7*30 is 210. 49 + 210 is 259. Correct. Then 328 - 259. Let's do it another way. 328 - 200 is 128. Then subtract 59 from 128. 128 - 50 is 78, 78 - 9 is 69. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by adding 259 and 69 to see if it equals 328. 259 + 60 is 319, plus 9 is 328. Perfect, that works. So the calculation checks out. \n\nTherefore, Ravi won't be able to place 69 almonds on the tables. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of almonds that won't fit is \\boxed{69}.\n</think>\n\nRavi has 328 almonds and 7 tables, each holding 37 almonds. To find out how many almonds he can't place on the tables, we first calculate the total capacity of the tables:\n\n\\[\n37 \\text{ almonds/table} \\times 7 \\text{ tables} = 259 \\text{ almonds}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the number of almonds Ravi has:\n\n\\[\n328 \\text{ almonds} - 259 \\text{ almonds} = 69 \\text{ almonds}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of almonds that won't fit on the tables is \\(\\boxed{69}\\).", "model_answer": 69.0, "ground_truth": 69.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 605, "question": "Oliver places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 35 cookies. If he has 338 cookies and 7 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Oliver places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 35 cookies. If he has 338 cookies and 7 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So the problem is about Oliver placing cookies on bowls. Each bowl can hold 35 cookies. He has 338 cookies and 7 bowls. The question is asking how many cookies he won't be able to place on the bowls. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of cookies he can put on the 7 bowls and then subtract that from the total number of cookies he has. That should give the number of cookies that don't fit, right?\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. Each bowl can hold 35 cookies. He has 7 bowls. So, the total number of cookies he can hold is 7 bowls multiplied by 35 cookies per bowl. Let me write that down:\n\nTotal cookies that can be placed = Number of bowls × Capacity per bowl\nTotal cookies that can be placed = 7 × 35\n\nLet me calculate that. 7 times 35. Hmm, 7 times 30 is 210, and 7 times 5 is 35. So adding those together, 210 + 35 = 245. So, he can place 245 cookies in total on the 7 bowls.\n\nBut he has 338 cookies. So, the number of cookies he can't place is the total cookies he has minus the total cookies he can place. That would be 338 - 245. Let me do that subtraction. 338 minus 200 is 138, then minus 45 more is 93. Wait, 245 + 93 = 338? Let me check that again. 245 + 90 is 335, and then +3 is 338. Yes, that's correct. So, 338 - 245 = 93.\n\nTherefore, the number of cookies he won't be able to place on the bowl is 93. Let me just verify my steps again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Calculated total capacity correctly: 7×35=245. Then subtracted the total cookies he has, 338-245=93. Seems straightforward. I don't see any errors here. So the answer should be 93.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of cookies that won't fit on the bowl is \\boxed{93}.\n</think>\n\n Oliver places cookies on 7 bowls, each holding 35 cookies. To find out how many cookies he can place in total, we multiply the number of bowls by the capacity of each bowl:\n\n\\[\n7 \\text{ bowls} \\times 35 \\text{ cookies per bowl} = 245 \\text{ cookies}\n\\]\n\nOliver has 338 cookies. The number of cookies that won't fit on the bowls is the total number of cookies minus the number that can be placed:\n\n\\[\n338 \\text{ total cookies} - 245 \\text{ cookies placed} = 93 \\text{ cookies}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{93}\n\\]", "model_answer": 93.0, "ground_truth": 93.0, "is_correct": true}
