kets. So 286 divided by 23. Let me do that division. 23 times 10 is 230, subtract that from 286, you get 56. Then 23 times 2 is 46, subtract that from 56, you get 10. So 10 divided by 23 is 0.434... So that would mean 10 bananas. So that's the same answer. So that seems to confirm it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using remainders. When you divide 286 by 23, the quotient is 12 with a remainder. So 23*12=276, and 286-276=10. So the remainder is 10. So that's the number that can't be placed. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be 10. But let me just make sure that there isn't a trick here. The problem says \"how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket.\" So it's the total number he has minus the number that can fit. So 286-276=10. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nI think that's it. So the answer is 10. But just to be thorough, let me check once more. 23*12=276. 286-276=10. Yep, that's correct. So 10 bananas won't fit. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas that won't fit is \\boxed{10}.\n</think>\n\nNoah has 286 bananas and 12 baskets, each capable of holding 23 bananas. To find out how many bananas won't fit, we first calculate the maximum number of bananas that can be placed in the baskets:\n\n\\[\n23 \\text{ bananas/basket} \\times 12 \\text{ baskets} = 276 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the number of bananas that can be placed from the total number of bananas Noah has:\n\n\\[\n286 \\text{ bananas} - 276 \\text{ bananas} = 10 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas that won't be able to fit on the baskets is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 10.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 608, "question": "Xavier places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 24 bananas. If he has 294 bananas and 12 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Xavier places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 24 bananas. If he has 294 bananas and 12 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Xavier has some bananas and some baskets. The problem is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm. Let me break this down.\n\nFirst, each basket can hold 24 bananas. He has 12 baskets. So, maybe I need to find out how many bananas he can hold in total with all the baskets. Then subtract the number of bananas he actually has, and the difference would be the ones he can't place. That makes sense.\n\nAlright, so total capacity would be the number of baskets multiplied by the capacity per basket. So 12 baskets times 24 bananas each. Let me calculate that. 12 times 24. Hmm, 10 times 24 is 240, and 2 times 24 is 48. So 240 plus 48 is 288. So the total capacity is 288 bananas.\n\nHe has 294 bananas. So now, subtract the number of bananas he has from the total capacity. 294 minus 288. Let me do that subtraction. 294 minus 288. Well, 288 plus 6 is 294, so the difference is 6. So he can't place 6 bananas.\n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Total capacity: 12 x 24. 12 x 20 is 240, 12 x 4 is 48. 240 + 48 is indeed 288. Then 294 bananas minus 288 is 6. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer should be 6 bananas he can't place. Hmm, seems straightforward. Let me think if there's another way to approach this problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe calculate how many bananas each basket can hold and how many he can put in each basket. Wait, but he has 294 bananas and 12 baskets. So if he distributes the bananas equally, each basket would have 294 divided by 12. Let me see. 294 divided by 12. 12 x 24 is 288, so 294 divided by 12 is 24.5. But you can't have half a banana, so he would have 24 bananas in each basket, which uses up 24 x 12 = 288 bananas, leaving 6 bananas left over. So that's the same answer. So either way, the answer is 6. Okay, that seems consistent.\n\nI think that's it. So the number of bananas he can't place is 6. Let me just make sure there's no trick or anything here. The problem says \"how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket,\" so it's straightforward subtraction. Total capacity minus actual bananas. Yep, that's it. So 288 - 294 = -6? Wait, no. Wait, 294 - 288 = 6. So he has 6 more bananas than the total capacity. Therefore, 6 bananas can't be placed. Yeah, that's correct. So the answer is 6. Alright, confident with that.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas Xavier can't place on the baskets is \\boxed{6}.\n</think>\n\nXavier places bananas on 12 baskets, with each basket holding 24 bananas. To find out how many bananas he can't place, we first calculate the total capacity of the baskets:\n\n\\[\n12 \\text{ baskets} \\times 24 \\text{ bananas per basket} = 288 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the number of bananas Xavier actually has from the total capacity:\n\n\\[\n294 \\text{ bananas} - 288 \\text{ bananas} = 6 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas Xavier can't place on the baskets is \\boxed{6}.", "model_answer": 6.0, "ground_truth": 6.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 609, "question": "Jin places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 29 olives. If he has 238 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Jin places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 29 olives. If he has 238 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Jin has 238 olives and 8 plates. Each plate can hold 29 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate can hold 29 olives and there are 8 plates, then the total capacity is 29 multiplied by 8. Let me do that multiplication. 29 times 8... 20 times 8 is 160, and 9 times 8 is 72, so adding those together gives 160 + 72 = 232. So the plates can hold a total of 232 olives. \n\nNow, Jin has 238 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 238 minus 232. Let me compute that. 238 minus 200 is 38, then minus 32 more would be 8. Wait, that seems low. Let me check again. 238 - 232. If I take away 200 from 238, I get 38. Then subtract the remaining 32, which is 38 - 32 = 6. Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, 232 is 200 + 32. So 238 - 232 is 6. Wait, 232 + 6 = 238. Yeah, that's correct. So 6 olives won't fit. \n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the multiplication. 29 times 8. Let's do it another way. 29 times 8. 10 times 8 is 80, 20 times 8 is 160, 9 times 8 is 72. Wait, no, that's not the right way. Wait, 29 is 20 + 9, so 20*8=160 and 9*8=72. Then 160 + 72 = 232. Yeah, that's correct. So total capacity is 232. \n\nThen 238 minus 232 is 6. So the answer should be 6. Hmm, but let me think again. Maybe I should check if there's another way to approach this problem. For example, how many plates would be needed to hold all the olives? If each plate can take 29, then dividing 238 by 29. Let's see, 29 times 8 is 232, which is less than 238. So he would need 9 plates to hold all of them. But he only has 8 plates. So the number of olives that can't be placed is 238 - (29*8) = 238 - 232 = 6. Yeah, that's the same answer. \n\nAlternatively, maybe think in terms of division with remainder. If you divide 238 by 29, the quotient is the number of plates fully filled, and the remainder is the number that can't fit. Let's see, 29*8=232, so 238 divided by 29 is 8 with a remainder of 6. So that's another way to see it. So the remainder is 6, which would be the number of olives that can't be placed. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 6. I think that's correct. Let me just verify once more. 8 plates times 29 olives each is 232. 238 minus 232 is 6. Yep, that's right. So Jin would have 6 olives left that he can't place on the plates. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of olives that won't fit is \\boxed{6}.\n</think>\n\nJin has 238 olives and 8 plates, each capable of holding 29 olives. To determine how many olives he cannot place on the plates, we first calculate the total capacity of the plates:\n\n\\[\n29 \\text{ olives/plate} \\times 8 \\text{ plates} = 232 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the number of olives Jin has:\n\n\\[\n238 \\text{ olives} - 232 \\text{ olives} = 6 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of olives that won't be able to place on the plate is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", "model_answer": 6.0, "ground_truth": 6.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 610, "question": "Jin places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 28 almonds. If he has 340 almonds and 10 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Jin places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 28 almonds. If he has 340 almonds and 10 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Jin has 340 almonds and 10 tables. Each table can hold 28 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so basically, we need to find out how many almonds he can place on the tables and then subtract that from the total he has, right?\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. Each table can hold 28 almonds. He has 10 tables. So the total number of almonds that can be placed on the tables is 10 tables multiplied by 28 almonds per table. Then, subtract that number from the total 340 almonds to find out how many are left over. That leftover is the number he can't place on the table.\n\nLet me write that down step by step. Total almonds: 340. Number of tables: 10. Almonds per table: 28. So total capacity is 10 times 28. Let me calculate that. 10 multiplied by 28. Well, 10 times 20 is 200, and 10 times 8 is 80. So 200 plus 80 is 280. So the tables can hold 280 almonds in total.\n\nNow, subtract that from the total number of almonds he has. So 340 minus 280. Let me do that subtraction. 340 minus 200 is 140, and then minus 80 more would be 60. So 340 minus 280 is 60. Therefore, he won't be able to place 60 almonds on the tables.\n\nWait, let me check my math again to be sure. 10 tables times 28 almonds each. 28 times 10 is indeed 280. Then 340 minus 280. Let's do it another way. 280 plus 60 is 340. So yes, 60 is the remainder. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as how many almonds each table can hold and how many tables there are. So 28 per table times 10 tables. 28*10=280. Total almonds 340. 340-280=60. Yep, same result.\n\nIs there another way to approach this problem? Maybe using division? Let's see. If he wants to distribute 340 almonds into 10 tables with 28 each, how many per table? So 340 divided by 10 is 34. Then 34 divided by 28 is... 34 divided by 28 is 1.214... So that's 1 and 6/28, which simplifies to 1 and 3/14. But that's the number of almonds per table. Wait, but maybe that's complicating it. The original approach seems straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using ratios. The ratio of total almonds to table capacity is 340:280. Simplify that ratio. Both numbers can be divided by 140. 340 divided by 140 is 2.428..., and 280 divided by 140 is 2. So the simplified ratio is 2.428:2, which is the same as 34:28. But I don't know if that helps here. The subtraction method is simpler.\n\nAnother check: 28 times 10 is 280. 340 minus 280 is 60. So 60 is the answer. That seems consistent. I think that's correct. Let me just verify once more. 10 tables, 28 each: 10*28=280. 340-280=60. Yep, that's right. So the number of almonds he can't place is 60. Therefore, the answer should be 60.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of almonds Jin won't be able to place on the table is \\boxed{60}.\n</think>\n\nJin has 340 almonds and 10 tables, each holding 28 almonds. To find out how many almonds he can't place on the tables, we first calculate the total number of almonds that can be placed on the tables:\n\n\\[\n10 \\text{ tables} \\times 28 \\text{ almonds per table} = 280 \\text{ almonds}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the number of almonds placed on the tables from the total number of almonds:\n\n\\[\n340 \\text{ total almonds} - 280 \\text{ almonds placed on tables} = 60 \\text{ almonds}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of almonds Jin won't be able to place on the table is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).", "model_answer": 60.0, "ground_truth": 60.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 611, "question": "Deshawn places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 29 bananas. If he has 347 bananas and 11 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Deshawn places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 29 bananas. If he has 347 bananas and 11 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So the problem is about Deshawn placing bananas on baskets. Each basket can hold 29 bananas. He has 347 bananas and 11 baskets. The question is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, right. So I need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can place on the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. That should give the number that can't fit. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, each basket can hold 29 bananas. He has 11 baskets. So the total number of bananas that can be held by all baskets together is 11 multiplied by 29. Let me calculate that. 11 times 29. Hmm, 10 times 29 is 290, and 1 more times 29 is 29, so 290 + 29 = 319. So 11 baskets can hold 319 bananas in total.\n\nBut he has 347 bananas. So if he tries to put all of them into the baskets, how many will be left over? That's the total capacity minus the number of bananas he has. So 347 minus 319. Let me do that subtraction. 347 - 319. Hmm, 347 minus 300 is 47, then minus 19 more is 28. So 347 - 319 = 28. Therefore, 28 bananas won't fit into the baskets.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. So 11 baskets times 29 bananas each. 11*29. Let me verify that multiplication. 29*10 is 290, plus 29 is 319. Yes, that's correct. Then 347 bananas minus 319 capacity. 347 - 319. Let's subtract 300 from 347, which gives 47, then subtract 19 more. 47 - 19 is 28. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as how many bananas each basket can take, and how many baskets can be filled completely, and then see if there are any remaining baskets or bananas. Let's try that approach. If each basket holds 29 bananas, how many baskets can be filled completely with 347 bananas? So divide 347 by 29. Let's see, 29*10 is 290, 29*12 is 348. Oh, wait, 29*12 is 348, which is just 1 more than 347. So that means 11 baskets can hold 11*29 = 319 bananas, and there's 347 - 319 = 28 bananas left. But wait, if he tries to put 12 baskets, each with 29 bananas, that would require 348 bananas, which he doesn't have. So he can only fill 11 baskets completely, and the 12th basket would have 28 bananas. But the question is about how many bananas he can't place. So regardless of how he arranges them, the total that can't be placed is 28. So that's the answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to think about it. If he has 11 baskets, each can take 29, so total capacity is 319. He has 347 bananas. So 347 - 319 = 28. So 28 bananas can't be placed. That seems straightforward. I think that's correct. Let me just check once more. 29*11: 29*10=290, 29*1=29, so 290+29=319. 347-319=28. Yep, that's right. So the answer should be 28. I don't see any mistakes here. So the number of bananas he can't place is 28.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas that won't fit is \\boxed{28}.\n</think>\n\nDeshawn places bananas on 11 baskets, each holding 29 bananas. To find out how many bananas he can't place, we first calculate the total capacity of the baskets:\n\n\\[\n11 \\text{ baskets} \\times 29 \\text{ bananas per basket} = 319 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total number of bananas he has from the total capacity:\n\n\\[\n347 \\text{ bananas} - 319 \\text{ bananas} = 28 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas that won't be able to fit on the baskets is \\(\\boxed{28}\\).", "model_answer": 28.0, "ground_truth": 28.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 612, "question": "Carlos places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 40 bananas. If he has 346 bananas and 7 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Carlos places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 40 bananas. If he has 346 bananas and 7 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Carlos has 346 bananas and 7 baskets. Each basket can hold 40 bananas. The question is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can put in the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. The difference should be the number that won't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the maximum bananas he can put in the baskets. Each basket holds 40 bananas, and there are 7 baskets. So, 40 bananas per basket times 7 baskets. Let me write that out: 40 * 7. Let me do the multiplication. 40 times 7 is 280. So, the total number of bananas he can hold is 280. \n\nNow, he has 346 bananas. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the maximum he can hold from the total he has. So, 346 minus 280. Let me compute that. 346 minus 200 is 146, then minus 80 more would be 66. Wait, 280 is 200 + 80. So, 346 - 200 = 146, then 146 - 80 = 66. So, 346 - 280 = 66. \n\nTherefore, Carlos won't be able to place 66 bananas on the baskets. Let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\n40 times 7: 40*7 is indeed 280. Then 346 minus 280: 346 - 280. Breaking it down, 300 - 200 = 100, 40 - 80. Wait, that might not be the best way. Let's do it step by step. 346 minus 280. Starting from 280, how much more do I need to get to 346? 280 + 60 = 340, so 340 is 60 more than 280. Then 340 + 6 = 346. So total is 60 + 6 = 66. Yeah, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, 346 - 280. Subtract 200 first: 346 - 200 = 146. Then subtract 80: 146 - 80. 146 - 60 = 86, then subtract 20 more: 86 - 20 = 66. Yep, same result. \n\nSo, the answer should be 66 bananas that won't fit. I think that's correct. Let me just verify once more. If each of the 7 baskets can take 40 bananas, then 7*40=280. Total bananas he has is 346. 346 - 280 = 66. Yep, that's right. \n\nI don't see any mistakes here. The key steps were multiplying the number of baskets by the capacity per basket to find the total that can be held, then subtracting that from the actual number of bananas he has. The result is the number that can't fit. So, the answer is 66.\n</think>\n\nCarlos can place a maximum of \\(40 \\text{ bananas/basket} \\times 7 \\text{ baskets} = 280 \\text{ bananas}\\) in the baskets. Subtracting this from the total number of bananas he has:\n\n\\[\n346 \\text{ total bananas} - 280 \\text{ bananas placed} = 66 \\text{ bananas left}\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{66}", "model_answer": 66.0, "ground_truth": 66.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 613, "question": "Wei places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 25 olives. If he has 239 olives and 7 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Wei places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 25 olives. If he has 239 olives and 7 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Wei has 239 olives and 7 plates. Each plate can hold 25 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. The difference should be the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate can hold 25 olives and there are 7 plates, then the total capacity is 25 multiplied by 7. Let me do that multiplication. 25 times 7... 25 times 7 is 175. So the plates can hold 175 olives in total. \n\nNow, Wei has 239 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 239 minus 175. Let me compute that. 239 minus 100 is 139, then minus 75 more would be 64. Wait, 175 is 100 + 75, so subtracting 100 gives 139, then subtract 75 from 139. 139 minus 70 is 69, then minus 5 is 64. So 239 minus 175 equals 64. \n\nTherefore, Wei won't be able to place 64 olives on the plates. Let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 25 times 7: 25*7 is indeed 175. Then 239 minus 175: 239 - 175. Let's do it another way. 175 + 64 = 239. Yes, because 175 + 60 is 235, and then +4 is 239. So that checks out. \n\nSo the answer should be 64 olives that can't be placed on the plates.\n</think>\n\nWei can place a maximum of \\(25 \\text{ olives/plate} \\times 7 \\text{ plates} = 175 \\text{ olives}\\) across all plates. With 239 olives, the number that won't fit is calculated by subtracting the total capacity from the total olives:\n\n\\[\n239 - 175 = 64\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{64}", "model_answer": 64.0, "ground_truth": 64.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 614, "question": "Sanjay places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 22 cookies. If he has 296 cookies and 13 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Sanjay places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 22 cookies. If he has 296 cookies and 13 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Sanjay has 296 cookies and 13 bowls. Each bowl can hold 22 cookies. The question is asking how many cookies he won't be able to place on the bowls. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of cookies he can put on the bowls and then subtract that from the total number of cookies he has. That should give the number of cookies that don't fit, right?\n\nFirst, let me calculate the total number of cookies that can be held by all the bowls. Since each bowl can hold 22 cookies and there are 13 bowls, I should multiply 22 by 13. Let me do that step by step. 22 times 10 is 220, and 22 times 3 is 66. Adding those together, 220 + 66 equals 286. So, the total capacity is 286 cookies.\n\nNow, he has 296 cookies. To find out how many cookies won't fit, I subtract the total capacity from the number of cookies he has. That would be 296 minus 286. Let me compute that. 296 minus 286 is 10. So, he can't place 10 cookies on the bowls.\n\nWait, let me double-check my math to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 22 times 13: 22 times 10 is 220, 22 times 3 is 66. 220 + 66 is indeed 286. Then 296 minus 286 is 10. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer should be 10 cookies that can't be placed on the bowl.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of cookies Sanjay won't be able to place on the bowl is \\boxed{10}.\n</think>\n\nSanjay has 296 cookies and 13 bowls, each capable of holding 22 cookies. To find out how many cookies won't fit, we first calculate the total capacity of the bowls:\n\n\\[\n22 \\text{ cookies/bowl} \\times 13 \\text{ bowls} = 286 \\text{ cookies}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the number of cookies Sanjay has:\n\n\\[\n296 \\text{ cookies} - 286 \\text{ cookies} = 10 \\text{ cookies}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of cookies Sanjay won't be able to place on the bowl is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 10.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 615, "question": "Esteban places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 21 almonds. If he has 236 almonds and 10 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Esteban places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 21 almonds. If he has 236 almonds and 10 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Esteban has 236 almonds and 10 tables. Each table can hold 21 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of almonds he can put on the tables and then subtract that from the total he has. The difference should be the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the tables. If each table holds 21 almonds and there are 10 tables, then the total capacity is 21 multiplied by 10. Let me write that down: 21 × 10. That's straightforward. 21 times 10 is 210. So, the tables can hold a maximum of 210 almonds altogether.\n\nNow, Esteban has 236 almonds. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of almonds he has. So that's 236 minus 210. Let me do that subtraction. 236 minus 200 is 36, and then minus 10 more would be 26. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 210 plus 26 is 236, yes. So 236 minus 210 equals 26. \n\nTherefore, Esteban won't be able to place 26 almonds on the tables. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. The tables can hold 21 each, 10 tables make 210. He has 236, so 236 - 210 = 26. Yep, that seems correct. I don't think there's any trick or anything here. It's a straightforward subtraction after calculating the total capacity. So the answer should be 26.\n</think>\n\nEsteban can place a maximum of \\(21 \\times 10 = 210\\) almonds on the tables. With 236 almonds, the number that won't fit is \\(236 - 210 = 26\\).\n\n\\boxed{26}", "model_answer": 26.0, "ground_truth": 26.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 616, "question": "Hiroshi places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 31 crackers. If he has 332 crackers and 9 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Hiroshi places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 31 crackers. If he has 332 crackers and 9 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Hiroshi has 332 crackers and 9 trays. Each tray can hold 31 crackers. The question is asking how many crackers he won't be able to place on the trays. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of crackers he can place on the trays and then subtract that from the total number of crackers he has. That should give me the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the trays. Since each tray holds 31 crackers and there are 9 trays, I can multiply 31 by 9. Let me do that. 31 times 9... 30 times 9 is 270, and 1 times 9 is 9, so 270 + 9 = 279. So the total capacity is 279 crackers. \n\nNow, he has 332 crackers. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the capacity from the total number of crackers. So 332 minus 279. Let me compute that. 332 - 200 is 132, then subtract 79 more. 132 - 70 is 62, and then subtract 9, which is 53. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check again. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 332 - 279. Let's subtract the hundreds first: 300 - 200 = 100. Then the tens: 30 - 70. Hmm, that's negative. So maybe I need to borrow. Let's do it step by step. \n\nStarting with 332 - 279:\n\n- Units place: 2 - 9. Can't do that, so borrow 1 from the tens place. But the tens place is 3, which becomes 2, and the units become 12. 12 - 9 = 3.\n\n- Tens place: 2 (after borrowing) - 7. Again, can't do that, so borrow 1 from the hundreds place. The hundreds place is 3, which becomes 2, and the tens become 12. 12 - 7 = 5.\n\n- Hundreds place: 2 (after borrowing) - 2 = 0.\n\nSo putting it all together: 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (units) → 053. So 53. Wait, that's the same as before. So 332 - 279 = 53. So he can't place 53 crackers. \n\nBut let me verify once more to be sure. If each of the 9 trays can hold 31, then 9*31=279. Total crackers he has is 332. 332 - 279 = 53. Yes, that seems correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by adding 279 + 53 to see if it equals 332. 279 + 50 = 329, then +3 = 332. Yes, that works. So 53 is the correct number. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be 53. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of crackers that won't fit is \\boxed{53}.\n</think>\n\nHiroshi has 332 crackers and 9 trays, each holding 31 crackers. To find out how many crackers he cannot place on the trays, we first calculate the total capacity of the trays:\n\n\\[\n9 \\text{ trays} \\times 31 \\text{ crackers per tray} = 279 \\text{ crackers}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the total number of crackers he has:\n\n\\[\n332 \\text{ crackers} - 279 \\text{ crackers} = 53 \\text{ crackers}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of crackers that won't fit on the trays is \\(\\boxed{53}\\).", "model_answer": 53.0, "ground_truth": 53.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 617, "question": "Faisal places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 20 crackers. If he has 205 crackers and 7 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Faisal places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 20 crackers. If he has 205 crackers and 7 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Faisal has 205 crackers and 7 trays. Each tray can hold 20 crackers. The question is asking how many crackers he won't be able to place on the trays. Hmm, right. So I need to figure out the maximum number of crackers he can place on the trays and then subtract that from the total number of crackers he has. That should give me the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the trays. Since each tray holds 20 crackers and there are 7 trays, I multiply 20 by 7. Let me do that. 20 times 7 is... 140. So the trays can hold 140 crackers in total. \n\nNow, he has 205 crackers. If the trays can hold 140, then the number of crackers that won't fit is 205 minus 140. Let me subtract that. 205 minus 140 is... 65. So, 65 crackers won't be able to place on the trays. \n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake. So 7 trays times 20 crackers each is indeed 140. Then 205 total crackers minus 140 that can be placed equals 65. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it another way. If each tray can hold 20, how many trays would he need to hold all 205 crackers? Let's see. Dividing 205 by 20. 20 times 10 is 200, so that's 10 trays for 200 crackers. Then there's 5 left over. So he would need 11 trays in total. But he only has 7 trays. So the number of trays he can actually use is 7. So 7 trays times 20 crackers is 140, same as before. Then 205 minus 140 is 65. Yep, same answer. \n\nAnother way: Maybe calculate how many crackers each tray can take, multiply by the number of trays, subtract from total. Which is exactly what I did first. So both methods give me 65. That gives me more confidence. \n\nI think that's solid. So the answer should be 65. Let me just make sure there's no trick or anything. The problem says \"how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray.\" So it's the total he has minus the ones that fit. Yep, straightforward. No hidden steps. So 65 is the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of crackers that won't fit is \\boxed{65}.\n</think>\n\nFaisal has 205 crackers and 7 trays, each holding 20 crackers. To find out how many crackers won't fit on the trays, we first calculate the total capacity of the trays:\n\n\\[\n7 \\text{ trays} \\times 20 \\text{ crackers per tray} = 140 \\text{ crackers}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the total number of crackers:\n\n\\[\n205 \\text{ crackers} - 140 \\text{ crackers} = 65 \\text{ crackers}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of crackers that won't be able to place on the tray is \\(\\boxed{65}\\).", "model_answer": 65.0, "ground_truth": 65.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 618, "question": "Diego places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 26 bananas. If he has 244 bananas and 9 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Diego places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 26 bananas. If he has 244 bananas and 9 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Diego has 244 bananas and 9 baskets. Each basket can hold 26 bananas. The question is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can put in the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. The difference should be the number that won't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the baskets. If each basket can hold 26 bananas and there are 9 baskets, then the total capacity is 26 multiplied by 9. Let me do that multiplication. 26 times 9... 20 times 9 is 180, and 6 times 9 is 54, so adding those together gives 180 + 54 = 234. So the baskets can hold a total of 234 bananas. \n\nNow, Diego has 244 bananas. To find out how many bananas he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of bananas he has. That would be 244 minus 234. Let me compute that. 244 minus 200 is 44, then minus 34 more would be 10. Wait, 244 - 234 is 10? Let me check that again. 234 + 10 = 244, yes, that's correct. So the answer should be 10 bananas that won't fit into the baskets. \n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. Let me verify each step. The total capacity: 9 baskets times 26 bananas each. 9*26. Breaking it down: 10 baskets would hold 260 bananas, but since there are only 9, that's 260 - 26 = 234. That checks out. Then 244 total bananas minus 234 capacity equals 10. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as how many bananas each basket can take, and how many baskets are filled. 26 bananas per basket, 9 baskets. So 9 baskets can hold 26*9=234. Then 244-234=10. Yep, same result. \n\nI think that's correct. So the number of bananas that won't fit is 10. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas that won't be able to be placed on the basket is \\boxed{10}.\n</think>\n\nDiego has 244 bananas and 9 baskets, each capable of holding 26 bananas. To determine how many bananas he cannot place, we first calculate the total capacity of the baskets:\n\n\\[\n26 \\, \\text{bananas/basket} \\times 9 \\, \\text{baskets} = 234 \\, \\text{bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the number of bananas Diego has:\n\n\\[\n244 \\, \\text{bananas} - 234 \\, \\text{bananas} = 10 \\, \\text{bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas that won't be able to be placed on the baskets is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 10.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 619, "question": "Pavel places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 32 olives. If he has 315 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Pavel places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 32 olives. If he has 315 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Pavel has 315 olives and 8 plates, each can hold 32 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can place on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. Each plate holds 32 olives, and there are 8 plates. So, multiplying 32 by 8 should give the total number of olives that can be placed. Let me do that: 32 times 8. Let's see, 30 times 8 is 240, and 2 times 8 is 16, so adding those together gives 240 + 16 = 256. So the plates can hold 256 olives in total.\n\nNow, Pavel has 315 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 315 minus 256. Let me compute that. 315 minus 200 is 115, then minus 56 more. 115 minus 50 is 65, and then minus 6 is 59. Wait, that seems right? Let me check again. 256 + 59 = 315? Let's add 256 + 50 first, which is 306, then add 9 more to get 315. Yes, that works. So 315 - 256 = 59. \n\nTherefore, Pavel won't be able to place 59 olives on the plates. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. The plates can hold 256, he has 315. 315 minus 256 is indeed 59. Yeah, that seems correct. I don't think there's any trick here, just straightforward subtraction after calculating total capacity. So the answer should be 59.\n</think>\n\nPavel can place a total of \\(32 \\text{ olives/plate} \\times 8 \\text{ plates} = 256 \\text{ olives}\\) across all plates. Since he has 315 olives, the number that won't fit is \\(315 - 256 = 59\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{59}\\)", "model_answer": 59.0, "ground_truth": 59.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 620, "question": "Thiago places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. If he has 209 bananas and 8 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Thiago places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. If he has 209 bananas and 8 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So the problem is about Thiago placing bananas on baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. He has 209 bananas and 8 baskets. The question is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, right. So I need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can put in the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. The difference should be the number that can't fit. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, each basket can hold 20 bananas. He has 8 baskets. So the total number of bananas that can be placed on the baskets is 8 baskets multiplied by 20 bananas per basket. Let me write that out: 8 * 20. Let me calculate that. 8 times 20 is 160. So 160 bananas can be placed in the baskets. But he has 209 bananas in total. So now I need to find out how many bananas are left after placing 160 of them. That would be 209 minus 160. Let me do that subtraction. 209 minus 160. Hmm, 200 minus 160 is 40, and then there's an extra 9, so 40 + 9 = 49. So 49 bananas left. Therefore, he won't be able to place 49 bananas on the baskets. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nTotal capacity: 8 baskets * 20 bananas/basket = 160. Total bananas he has: 209. So 209 - 160 = 49. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer should be 49. But let me think if there's another way to approach this problem. Maybe using division? If he wants to distribute 209 bananas into 8 baskets, each with 20 per bas