{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 622, "question": "Alejandro places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. If he has 304 bananas and 9 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Alejandro places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. If he has 304 bananas and 9 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So the problem is that Alejandro has 304 bananas and 9 baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. We need to find out how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, right. So basically, we need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can put in the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. The difference should be the number that won't fit. \n\nFirst, let me confirm the numbers. Each basket can hold 20 bananas, and he has 9 baskets. So the total capacity would be 9 baskets multiplied by 20 bananas per basket. Let me calculate that. 9 times 20... 9*20 is 180. So the baskets can hold 180 bananas in total. \n\nBut he has 304 bananas. So if he tries to put all of them into the baskets, how many will be left over? That's the key here. So we need to divide the total number of bananas by the total capacity of the baskets. So 304 divided by 180. Let me do that division. \n\n304 divided by 180. Hmm, 180 goes into 304 once, because 180*1 is 180. Subtracting that from 304, we get 124. Then, 180 goes into 124 zero times, so we have a remainder of 124. So that means after filling up all the baskets as much as possible, there will be 124 bananas left that can't fit into the baskets. \n\nWait, let me check that again. If each basket holds 20, 9 baskets hold 180. 304 bananas minus 180 is 124. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer should be 124. \n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the division. Let's do the division step by step. 180 times 1 is 180. 304 minus 180 is 124. Then, 180 goes into 124 zero times with a remainder of 124. So yes, the remainder is 124. Therefore, 124 bananas won't be able to be placed on the baskets. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as how many bananas each basket can take. If he puts 20 bananas in each basket, how many baskets can he fill completely? 304 divided by 20. Let's see, 20 times 15 is 300. So 15 baskets would hold 300 bananas, and the 16th basket would have 4 bananas. Wait, but he only has 9 baskets. So he can fill all 9 baskets with 20 bananas each, which is 180. Then, the remaining bananas are 304 - 180 = 124. So that's the same answer. \n\nSo both methods give me 124. So that must be the correct answer. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use multiplication. 20 times 9 is 180. 304 minus 180 is 124. Yep. So regardless of the approach, the answer is 124. \n\nI think that's solid. So the number of bananas that won't fit is 124.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas that won't be able to be placed on the basket is \\boxed{124}.\n</think>\n\nAlejandro has 304 bananas and 9 baskets, each capable of holding 20 bananas. To find out how many bananas he won't be able to place, we first calculate the total capacity of the baskets:\n\n\\[\n9 \\text{ baskets} \\times 20 \\text{ bananas per basket} = 180 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine how many bananas will remain after filling the baskets to capacity:\n\n\\[\n304 \\text{ total bananas} - 180 \\text{ bananas that fit} = 124 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas that won't be able to be placed on the basket is \\(\\boxed{124}\\).", "model_answer": 124.0, "ground_truth": 124.0, "is_correct": true}
