n take 32, 9 baskets can take 9*32=288. Total bananas are 308. 308-288=20. Yes, that seems correct. So the answer should be 20. \n\nBut just to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in multiplication. 32*9: 32*10 is 320, subtract 32 gives 288. Correct. Then 308-288=20. Yep, that's right. So the number of bananas that won't fit is 20. \n\nI think that's it. It's straightforward once you calculate the total capacity and subtract from the total number of bananas. No complicated steps here. Just multiplication and subtraction. So the answer is 20.\n</think>\n\nLeon can place a total of \\(32 \\times 9 = 288\\) bananas across the 9 baskets. Subtracting this from the total number of bananas he has:  \n\\[\n308 - 288 = 20\n\\]  \nThus, the number of bananas he won't be able to place is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).", "model_answer": 20.0, "ground_truth": 20.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 625, "question": "Sanjay places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 23 almonds. If he has 276 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Sanjay places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 23 almonds. If he has 276 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Sanjay has 276 almonds and 7 tables. Each table can hold 23 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of almonds he can put on the tables and then subtract that from the total he has. The difference between the total and what he can hold will be the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the tables. If each table holds 23 almonds and there are 7 tables, then the total capacity is 23 multiplied by 7. Let me do that multiplication. 23 times 7... 20 times 7 is 140, and 3 times 7 is 21, so adding those together gives 140 + 21 = 161. So the tables can hold a total of 161 almonds. \n\nNow, Sanjay has 276 almonds. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of almonds he has. So that's 276 minus 161. Let me compute that. 276 minus 160 would be 116, and then minus 1 more is 115. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 276 - 161. Breaking it down: 200 - 100 = 100, 70 - 60 = 10, 6 - 1 = 5. Adding those together: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115. Yeah, that seems correct. \n\nSo, Sanjay can't place 115 almonds on the tables. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes in my calculations. 23 times 7: 23*7. 20*7=140, 3*7=21, 140+21=161. Correct. Then 276-161=115. Yes, that's right. So the answer should be 115. \n\nI think that's all. The key steps were calculating the total capacity first and then subtracting the number of almonds he has from that capacity. The result is the number that doesn't fit. No, I don't see any errors here. So the final answer is 115.\n</think>\n\nSanjay can place a maximum of \\(23 \\text{ almonds/table} \\times 7 \\text{ tables} = 161 \\text{ almonds}\\) on the tables. With 276 almonds, the number that won't fit is \\(276 - 161 = 115\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{115}\\)", "model_answer": 115.0, "ground_truth": 115.0, "is_correct": true}
