{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 627, "question": "Liam places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 36 almonds. If he has 316 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Liam places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 36 almonds. If he has 316 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Liam has 316 almonds and 7 tables. Each table can hold 36 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of almonds he can place on the tables and then subtract that from the total number he has. The difference between the total and the maximum placed should be the answer.\n\nFirst, let me confirm the numbers. Each table holds 36 almonds, and there are 7 tables. So, the total number of almonds that can be placed on the tables is 36 multiplied by 7. Let me calculate that. 36 times 7... 30 times 7 is 210, and 6 times 7 is 42. Adding those together, 210 + 42 = 252. So, 252 almonds can be placed on the tables.\n\nNow, Liam has 316 almonds. To find out how many he can't place on the tables, I subtract the maximum number he can place (252) from the total he has (316). So, 316 minus 252. Let me do that subtraction. 316 - 200 is 116, then subtract 52 more. 116 - 52 = 64. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 252 + 64 = 316? Let's add 252 + 60 = 312, then +4 = 316. Yes, that works. So, 316 - 252 = 64.\n\nTherefore, Liam won't be able to place 64 almonds on the tables. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. Each table is 36, 7 tables, 36*7=252. Total almonds 316. 316-252=64. Yep, that seems correct. I don't think there's any trick here. It's straightforward subtraction after calculating the maximum capacity. So the answer should be 64.\n</think>\n\nLiam can place a maximum of \\(36 \\text{ almonds/table} \\times 7 \\text{ tables} = 252 \\text{ almonds}\\). Subtracting this from the total number of almonds he has:  \n\\[\n316 - 252 = 64\n\\]  \nThus, **64 almonds** will not fit on the tables.\n\n\\boxed{64}", "model_answer": 64.0, "ground_truth": 64.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 628, "question": "Jamal places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 26 olives. If he has 257 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Jamal places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 26 olives. If he has 257 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Jamal has 257 olives and 8 plates. Each plate can hold 26 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate holds 26 olives and there are 8 plates, then the total capacity is 26 multiplied by 8. Let me do that multiplication. 26 times 8... 20 times 8 is 160, and 6 times 8 is 48, so adding those together gives 160 + 48 = 208. So the plates can hold a total of 208 olives. \n\nNow, he has 257 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 257 minus 208. Let me do that subtraction. 257 minus 200 is 57, and then minus 8 more would be 49. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 208 + 49 = 257. Yes, because 208 + 40 is 248, and 248 +9 is 257. So that's correct. \n\nTherefore, the number of olives that won't fit is 49. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. So each plate holds 26, 8 plates, 26*8=208. Total olives 257. 257-208=49. Yep, that seems right. I don't think there's any trick here. It's straightforward multiplication and subtraction. So the answer should be 49.\n</think>\n\nJamal can place a maximum of \\(26 \\times 8 = 208\\) olives across all 8 plates. With 257 olives, the number that cannot fit is calculated by subtracting the total capacity from the total number of olives:\n\n\\[\n257 - 208 = 49\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{49}", "model_answer": 49.0, "ground_truth": 49.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 629, "question": "Andre places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. If he has 326 bananas and 8 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Andre places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. If he has 326 bananas and 8 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Andre has 326 bananas and 8 baskets. Each basket can hold 20 bananas. The question is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can put in the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. The difference should be the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the maximum number of bananas that can go into the baskets. Since each basket holds 20 bananas and there are 8 baskets, I can multiply 20 by 8. Let me do that: 20 times 8 is 160. So, 160 bananas can fit into the baskets. \n\nNow, he has 326 bananas in total. To find out how many won't fit, I subtract the number that can be placed from the total number he has. So that's 326 minus 160. Let me compute that. 326 minus 160... Well, 326 minus 100 is 226, then minus 60 more would be 166. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 160 plus 160 is 320, so 326 minus 320 is 6. Wait, no, that doesn't make sense. Wait, 160 plus 60 is 220, so 326 minus 220 is 106. Hmm, maybe I messed up the subtraction steps. Let me try another way. 326 minus 100 is 226, then subtract 60 more. 226 minus 60 is 166. Wait, but 160 plus 166 is 326? Let me check: 160 + 100 is 260, plus 60 is 320. So 160 + 166 is 326. Yes, that's correct. So 326 minus 160 is 166. \n\nWait, but hold on. Let me verify with another method. If each basket can take 20 bananas, then 8 baskets can take 8*20=160. So total capacity is 160. He has 326 bananas. So 326 - 160 = 166. So 166 bananas cannot be placed. That seems straightforward. \n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in multiplication. 20 times 8: 20*8 is 160. Correct. Then 326-160. Let's subtract 160 from 326. 326 minus 100 is 226, minus 60 is 166. Yes, that's correct. So the answer should be 166. \n\nWait, but let me think again. Maybe there's a trick here. The problem says \"how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket\". So each basket can hold 20, so if he puts 20 in each basket, he can fill 8 baskets. 8*20=160. Then 326-160=166. So 166 bananas can't be placed. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is asking per basket? Like, how many bananas in total can't be placed on any basket? But that would still be the same as total capacity minus total bananas. So 160-326=166. Wait, no, 326-160=166. Yeah, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe per basket, how many can't be placed? But that would be if each basket had less than 20. But since he can fill all baskets with 20 each, the total that can't be placed is just the difference. \n\nSo I think the answer is 166. Let me check once more. 8 baskets * 20 bananas per basket = 160. Total bananas are 326. 326 - 160 = 166. Yep, that's correct. So the number of bananas that won't fit is 166. \n\nI don't see any other way to interpret the problem. The key is that each basket can hold 20, so the total capacity is 160. He has 326, so 326-160=166. That's straightforward. So the answer is 166.\n</think>\n\nAndre can place a maximum of \\(8 \\text{ baskets} \\times 20 \\text{ bananas per basket} = 160 \\text{ bananas}\\) in total. Since he has 326 bananas, the number that won't fit is calculated by subtracting the total capacity from the total number of bananas:\n\n\\[\n326 - 160 = 166\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas Andre won't be able to place on the baskets is \\(\\boxed{166}\\).", "model_answer": 166.0, "ground_truth": 166.0, "is_correct": true}
