late, and the rest would be 0. But the total capacity is 168, but he only has 21. So, the number he can't place is 21-0=21? That doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe the problem is different. \n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that he has 270 olives and 8 plates, each can hold 21. So, the maximum number he can place is 8*21=168. Therefore, the number he can't place is 270-168=102. That seems straightforward. \n\nAlternatively, if he tried to put as many as possible on each plate, he would put 33 on each plate (since 33*8=264), leaving 6. But the problem is not about distributing all the olives, but about how many he can't place. So, even if he only puts 33 on each plate, he still can't place the remaining 6. But the total capacity is 168, so regardless of how he distributes them, he", "model_answer": 168.0, "ground_truth": 102.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 631, "question": "Umar places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 26 crackers. If he has 313 crackers and 7 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Umar places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 26 crackers. If he has 313 crackers and 7 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Umar has 313 crackers and 7 trays. Each tray can hold 26 crackers. The question is asking how many crackers he won't be able to place on the trays. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of crackers he can place on the trays and then subtract that from the total number of crackers he has. That should give me the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the trays. Since each tray holds 26 crackers and there are 7 trays, I can multiply 26 by 7. Let me do that. 26 times 7... 20 times 7 is 140, and 6 times 7 is 42, so adding those together gives 140 + 42 = 182. So the trays can hold a total of 182 crackers. \n\nNow, Umar has 313 crackers. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of crackers he has. So that's 313 minus 182. Let me compute that. 313 minus 100 is 213, minus 80 more is 133, and then minus 2 more is 131. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check again. 313 - 182. Maybe breaking it down differently: 182 + 131 = 313? Let's see, 182 + 100 = 282, then +31 is 313. Yes, that's correct. So 313 - 182 = 131. \n\nTherefore, Umar won't be able to place 131 crackers on the trays. Let me just verify once more to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 7 trays times 26 crackers per tray: 7*26. 7*20=140, 7*6=42, 140+42=182. Total capacity 182. Total crackers 313. 313-182=131. Yep, that seems right. \n\nI think that's all. The key steps were calculating the total capacity and then subtracting the total crackers from that capacity. The answer should be 131.\n</think>\n\nUmar can place a maximum of \\(7 \\times 26 = 182\\) crackers on the trays. With 313 crackers, the number that won't fit is \\(313 - 182 = 131\\).\n\n\\boxed{131}", "model_answer": 131.0, "ground_truth": 131.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 632, "question": "Arash places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 39 crackers. If he has 302 crackers and 7 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Arash places crackers on some trays. Each tray can hold 39 crackers. If he has 302 crackers and 7 trays, how many crackers won't he be able to place on the tray?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Arash has 302 crackers and 7 trays. Each tray can hold 39 crackers. The question is asking how many crackers he won't be able to place on the trays. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of crackers he can place on the trays and then subtract that from the total number of crackers he has. That should give me the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the trays. Since each tray holds 39 crackers and there are 7 trays, I can multiply 39 by 7. Let me do that. 39 times 7... Let's see, 30 times 7 is 210, and 9 times 7 is 63. Adding those together gives 210 + 63 = 273. So the total capacity is 273 crackers. \n\nNow, he has 302 crackers. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the capacity from the total number of crackers. So 302 minus 273. Let me compute that. 302 minus 200 is 102, then minus 73 more. 102 - 73. Hmm, 102 minus 70 is 32, and then minus 3 is 29. So 302 - 273 = 29. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 39 times 7: 30*7=210, 9*7=63, 210+63=273. That's correct. Then 302-273. Let's do it another way. 273 + 29 = 302. Yes, because 273 + 20 = 293, then +9 = 302. So the difference is indeed 29. \n\nTherefore, Arash won't be able to place 29 crackers on the trays. \n\nI think that's it. Let me just verify once more. If each of the 7 trays can take 39, then 7*39=273. Total crackers are 302. 302-273=29. Yep, that seems right. No, I don't see any errors here. So the answer should be 29.\n</think>\n\nArash has 302 crackers and 7 trays, each holding 39 crackers. To find the number of crackers he can't place:\n\n1. Calculate total capacity:  \n   \\( 39 \\times 7 = 273 \\) crackers.\n\n2. Subtract from total crackers:  \n   \\( 302 - 273 = 29 \\).\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{29}", "model_answer": 29.0, "ground_truth": 29.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 633, "question": "Tariq places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 20 cookies. If he has 259 cookies and 12 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Tariq places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 20 cookies. If he has 259 cookies and 12 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Tariq has 259 cookies and 12 bowls. Each bowl can hold 20 cookies. The question is asking how many cookies he won't be able to place on the bowls. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of cookies he can put on the bowls and then subtract that from the total number of cookies he has. That should give me the number of cookies that don't fit, right?\n\nFirst, let me calculate the maximum number of cookies he can place on the bowls. Since each bowl can hold 20 cookies and there are 12 bowls, I should multiply the number of bowls by the number of cookies per bowl. So that's 12 bowls times 20 cookies per bowl. Let me do that multiplication. 12 times 20... 12 times 2 is 24, so 12 times 20 is 240. So, 240 cookies can be placed on the bowls.\n\nNow, he has 259 cookies in total. To find out how many cookies won't fit, I subtract the number of cookies that can be placed on the bowls from the total number of cookies. So that's 259 minus 240. Let me do that subtraction. 259 minus 240 is... 19. Wait, 240 plus 19 is 259, so yes, that's correct. So, 19 cookies won't fit on the bowls.\n\nLet me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 12 bowls times 20 cookies each: 12*20. 10 bowls would hold 200 cookies, and 2 more bowls would hold 40, so 200+40=240. That's right. Total cookies are 259, so 259-240=19. Yep, that seems correct. So the answer should be 19 cookies that can't be placed on the bowls.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of cookies Tariq won't be able to place on the bowl is \\boxed{19}.\n</think>\n\nTariq has 259 cookies and 12 bowls, each capable of holding 20 cookies. To find the number of cookies that won't fit on the bowls, we first calculate the maximum number of cookies that can be placed on the bowls:\n\n\\[\n12 \\text{ bowls} \\times 20 \\text{ cookies per bowl} = 240 \\text{ cookies}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the number of cookies that can be placed on the bowls from the total number of cookies:\n\n\\[\n259 \\text{ total cookies} - 240 \\text{ cookies placed on bowls} = 19 \\text{ cookies}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of cookies Tariq won't be able to place on the bowl is \\(\\boxed{19}\\).", "model_answer": 19.0, "ground_truth": 19.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 634, "question": "Viktor places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 23 cookies. If he has 286 cookies and 7 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Viktor places cookies on some bowls. Each bowl can hold 23 cookies. If he has 286 cookies and 7 bowls, how many cookies won't he be able to place on the bowl?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Viktor has 286 cookies and 7 bowls. Each bowl can hold 23 cookies. The question is asking how many cookies he won't be able to place on the bowls. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of cookies he can put on the bowls and then subtract that from the total number of cookies he has. That should give the number of cookies that don't fit, right?\n\nFirst, let me calculate the total number of cookies that can be held by all the bowls. Since each bowl can hold 23 cookies and there are 7 bowls, I should multiply 23 by 7. Let me do that step by step. 23 times 7... Well, 20 times 7 is 140, and 3 times 7 is 21. Adding those together, 140 + 21 = 161. So, the total capacity is 161 cookies.\n\nBut Viktor has 286 cookies. Since 286 is more than 161, he can't fit all of them. So the number of cookies that won't fit is 286 minus 161. Let me subtract that. 286 - 161. Hmm, 286 minus 160 would be 126, and then minus 1 more is 125. So, 125 cookies won't fit.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 23 times 7: 20*7=140, 3*7=21, 140+21=161. Correct. Then 286-161. Let's do it another way. 161 + 100 = 261. 286 - 261 = 25. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Wait, 286 - 161. Let's break it down:\n\n286 - 100 = 186\n\n186 - 60 = 126\n\n126 - 1 = 125\n\nYes, that's right. So 125. So the answer should be 125 cookies that can't be placed. Let me just verify once more. 7 bowls * 23 cookies per bowl = 161 total. 286 total cookies. 286 - 161 = 125. Yep, that seems correct. I don't think I made any mistakes here. So the answer is 125.\n</think>\n\nViktor can place a maximum of \\(7 \\times 23 = 161\\) cookies on the bowls. With 286 cookies, the number that won't fit is \\(286 - 161 = 125\\).\n\n\\boxed{125}", "model_answer": 125.0, "ground_truth": 125.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 635, "question": "Arjun places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 35 olives. If he has 305 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Arjun places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 35 olives. If he has 305 olives and 8 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Arjun has 305 olives and 8 plates, each can hold 35 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate holds 35 olives and there are 8 plates, then the total capacity is 35 multiplied by 8. Let me do that multiplication. 35 times 8... 30 times 8 is 240, and 5 times 8 is 40, so adding those together gives 240 + 40 = 280. So the plates can hold a total of 280 olives. \n\nNow, he has 305 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 305 minus 280. Let me compute that. 305 minus 200 is 105, then minus 80 more would be 25. Wait, 280 is 200 + 80, so 305 - 200 = 105, then 105 - 80 = 25. So 305 - 280 = 25. \n\nTherefore, Arjun won't be able to place 25 olives on the plates. Let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nTotal capacity: 35 x 8. 35 x 8 is indeed 280. Then 305 - 280 is 25. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer should be 25. \n\nWait, another way to think about it: If each plate can take 35, how many plates would be needed for 305? Let's divide 305 by 35. 35 x 10 is 350, which is more than 305. So 35 x 9 = 315. That's too much. So 9 plates would be needed, but he only has 8. So 9 plates needed, 8 available. So 9 - 8 = 1 plate is needed but not available. Wait, but each plate can hold 35. So maybe that's another way to approach it. \n\nWait, if he needs 9 plates to hold all 305, but he only has 8, then he can't use the 9th plate. So how many olives would be left after using 8 plates. Each plate holds 35, so 8 plates hold 8 x 35 = 280. Then 305 - 280 = 25. So same answer. \n\nSo both methods give 25. That seems consistent. So I think 25 is the correct answer. \n\nI don't see any mistakes here. The key steps are calculating total capacity and subtracting the number of olives. Alternatively, calculating the number of plates needed and subtracting the available plates. Both lead to the same result. So I'm confident the answer is 25.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of olives Arjun won't be able to place on the plates is \\boxed{25}.\n</think>\n\nArjun has 305 olives and 8 plates, each holding 35 olives. To find out how many olives he won't be able to place, we first calculate the total capacity of the plates:\n\n\\[\n35 \\text{ olives/plate} \\times 8 \\text{ plates} = 280 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has:\n\n\\[\n305 \\text{ olives} - 280 \\text{ olives} = 25 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of olives Arjun won't be able to place on the plates is \\boxed{25}.", "model_answer": 25.0, "ground_truth": 25.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 636, "question": "Bodhi places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 34 almonds. If he has 326 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Bodhi places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 34 almonds. If he has 326 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Bodhi has 326 almonds and 7 tables. Each table can hold 34 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of almonds he can put on the tables and then subtract that from the total he has. The difference between the total and the maximum he can place would be the number he can't place, right?\n\nFirst, let me confirm the numbers. Each table holds 34 almonds, and there are 7 tables. So, the total capacity is 34 times 7. Let me calculate that. 34 multiplied by 7. Let's do 30*7=210 and 4*7=28, so 210+28=238. So, the tables can hold 238 almonds in total.\n\nNow, he has 326 almonds. To find out how many he can't place, subtract the total capacity from the total he has. So, 326 minus 238. Let me do that subtraction. 326 - 200 is 126, then subtract 38 more. 126 - 38. Hmm, 126 - 30 is 96, then subtract 8 is 88. So, 326 - 238 = 88. Therefore, he won't be able to place 88 almonds on the tables.\n\nWait, let me check my math again to be sure. 34*7: 34*5=170, 34*2=68, so 170+68=238. That's correct. Then 326-238. Let's do it another way. 326 + 88 = 414. Wait, no, that's not helpful. Alternatively, 238 + 88 = 326. Wait, that's interesting. So 238 + 88 = 326. Therefore, 326 - 238 = 88. Yeah, that checks out. So the answer is 88. So he can't place 88 almonds.\n\nBut let me think again. Maybe there's another way to approach this. For example, how many almonds would he need per table? If each table can take 34, how many tables can he fill? 326 divided by 34. Let's see. 34*9=306. 34*10=340. So 326 is between 9 and 10 tables. 34*9=306, so 326-306=20. So he can fill 9 tables with 34 almonds each, which is 306, and then he has 20 left. But wait, each table can hold 34, so he can't put 20 into a single table. So he would need 10 tables for the remaining 20? But he only has 7 tables. Wait, but the question is about how many he can't place. So regardless of whether he can fit them or not, the total he can't place is 326 - (number of tables * 34). So even if he could use more tables, but he only has 7, the calculation is just total he has minus total capacity. So 326-238=88. So the answer is 88. So maybe my first calculation was correct.\n\nAlternatively, if he tried to distribute the 326 almonds as evenly as possible among 7 tables, each table would get 326 divided by 7. Let's see, 326 divided by 7. 7*46=322. So 326-322=4. So each table would have 46 almonds, and 4 tables would have 47. But the question isn't about how to distribute them, but how many he can't place. So regardless of how he arranges them, the total he can't place is 326 - (7*34)=88. So the answer is 88. So I think that's correct.\n\nBut let me check once more. 7 tables * 34 almonds = 238. 326 - 238 = 88. Yes. So the answer is 88. So I think that's right. So the number of almonds he can't place is 88.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of almonds Bodhi won't be able to place on the table is \\boxed{88}.\n</think>\n\nBodhi has 326 almonds and 7 tables, each capable of holding 34 almonds. To determine how many almonds he cannot place on the tables, we first calculate the total capacity of the tables:\n\n\\[\n34 \\text{ almonds/table} \\times 7 \\text{ tables} = 238 \\text{ almonds}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the total number of almonds he has:\n\n\\[\n326 \\text{ almonds} - 238 \\text{ almonds} = 88 \\text{ almonds}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of almonds Bodhi won't be able to place on the table is \\(\\boxed{88}\\).", "model_answer": 88.0, "ground_truth": 88.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 637, "question": "Ibrahim places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 21 bananas. If he has 245 bananas and 7 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Ibrahim places bananas on some baskets. Each basket can hold 21 bananas. If he has 245 bananas and 7 baskets, how many bananas won't he be able to place on the basket?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Ibrahim has 245 bananas and 7 baskets. Each basket can hold 21 bananas. The question is asking how many bananas he won't be able to place on the baskets. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of bananas he can put in the baskets and then subtract that from the total number of bananas he has. That should give the number of bananas that don't fit, right?\n\nFirst, let me calculate the maximum number of bananas that can go into the baskets. Since each basket can hold 21 bananas, and there are 7 baskets, I should multiply 21 by 7. Let me do that. 21 times 7... 20 times 7 is 140, and 1 times 7 is 7, so 140 + 7 = 147. So, the total number of bananas that can be placed in the baskets is 147.\n\nNow, Ibrahim has 245 bananas. To find out how many bananas he can't place, I subtract the number he can place from the total he has. So that's 245 minus 147. Let me compute that. 245 minus 100 is 145, then minus 47 more. 145 minus 40 is 105, and then minus 7 is 98. Wait, that seems a bit high. Let me check again. Alternatively, 245 - 147. Maybe break it down differently. 147 + 98 = 245? Let's see: 147 + 90 = 237, then +8 = 245. Yes, that works. So 245 - 147 = 98. So, 98 bananas won't fit into the baskets.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the multiplication. 21 times 7. 20*7=140, 1*7=7, so 140+7=147. That's correct. Then 245-147. Let me do it another way. 245 minus 140 is 105, then minus 7 more is 98. Yeah, that's the same result. So, 98 bananas can't be placed. Hmm, seems right. Let me just visualize it. If each basket takes 21, 7 baskets take 147. He has 245, so 245-147=98. Yep, that's the answer. I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas Ibrahim won't be able to place on the baskets is \\boxed{98}.\n</think>\n\nIbrahim has 245 bananas and 7 baskets, each capable of holding 21 bananas. To find out how many bananas he won't be able to place, we first calculate the maximum number of bananas that can be placed in the baskets:\n\n\\[\n21 \\text{ bananas/basket} \\times 7 \\text{ baskets} = 147 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the number of bananas that can be placed in the baskets from the total number of bananas Ibrahim has:\n\n\\[\n245 \\text{ bananas} - 147 \\text{ bananas} = 98 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas Ibrahim won't be able to place on the baskets is \\(\\boxed{98}\\).", "model_answer": 98.0, "ground_truth": 98.0, "is_correct": true}
