left over that can't fit. Therefore, the answer should be 55. \n\nI think that's all. Let me just recap to make sure. Total capacity is 34*8=272. Total crackers are 327. Subtract 272 from 327 gives 55. Yep, that seems correct. No steps skipped here. I don't see any errors in the calculations. So the number of crackers he can't place is 55.\n</think>\n\nEsteban can place a maximum of \\(34 \\text{ crackers/tray} \\times 8 \\text{ trays} = 272 \\text{ crackers}\\) on the trays. With a total of 327 crackers, the number that won't fit is calculated by subtracting the total capacity from the total number of crackers:\n\n\\[\n327 - 272 = 55\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{55}", "model_answer": 55.0, "ground_truth": 55.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 639, "question": "Tariq places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 23 olives. If he has 256 olives and 11 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Tariq places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 23 olives. If he has 256 olives and 11 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Tariq has 256 olives and 11 plates. Each plate can hold 23 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate can hold 23 olives and there are 11 plates, then the total capacity is 23 multiplied by 11. Let me do that multiplication. 23 times 10 is 230, and then 23 more would be 253. So, 23 x 11 = 253. That means all the plates together can hold 253 olives. \n\nNow, he has 256 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the total number of olives. So that's 256 minus 253. Let me compute that. 256 minus 250 is 6, and then minus 3 more is 3. So, 256 - 253 = 3. \n\nWait, that seems really low. Let me check again. If each plate can take 23, 11 plates would take 253. 253 + 3 = 256. So, yeah, 3 is the number that doesn't fit. That makes sense. So, Tariq can't place 3 olives on the plates. \n\nBut let me think again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe I should verify the multiplication first. 23 x 11. Breaking it down: 20 x 11 = 220, and 3 x 11 = 33. Then 220 + 33 = 253. Yep, that's correct. Then 256 - 253 = 3. Hmm, seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to approach this problem. For example, dividing the total number of olives by the number of plates. Let's see, 256 divided by 11. How many times does 11 go into 256? 11 x 23 = 253, which is the same as before. So, 256 divided by 11 is 23 with a remainder. The remainder is 256 - 253 = 3. So, that's another way to see it. \n\nSo, the remainder tells us how many olives can't be evenly distributed, meaning they can't fit on the plates. Therefore, the answer should be 3. \n\nI think that's solid. Both methods give the same result. So, I'm confident that 3 is the correct number of olives that won't fit.\n</think>\n\nTariq can place a maximum of \\(23 \\times 11 = 253\\) olives across the 11 plates. With a total of 256 olives, the number that cannot be placed is calculated by subtracting the total capacity from the total number of olives:\n\n\\[\n256 - 253 = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, Tariq won't be able to place \\(\\boxed{3}\\) olives on the plates.", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 3.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 640, "question": "Leon places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 27 almonds. If he has 251 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Leon places almonds on some tables. Each table can hold 27 almonds. If he has 251 almonds and 7 tables, how many almonds won't he be able to place on the table?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Leon has 251 almonds and 7 tables. Each table can hold 27 almonds. The question is asking how many almonds he won't be able to place on the tables. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of almonds he can put on the tables and then subtract that from the total he has. The difference between the total and the maximum he can place would be the almonds left over, right?\n\nFirst, let me confirm the numbers. Each table can hold 27 almonds. He has 7 tables. So, the total number of almonds that can be placed on the tables is 27 multiplied by 7. Let me calculate that. 27 times 7... 20 times 7 is 140, and 7 times 7 is 49. So 140 plus 49 is 189. So, 7 tables times 27 almonds each equals 189 almonds. That means he can place 189 almonds on the tables.\n\nBut he has 251 almonds in total. So, if he uses 189 almonds, how many will be left? That's 251 minus 189. Let me do that subtraction. 251 minus 180 is 71, and then minus 9 more is 62. So, 251 minus 189 equals 62. Therefore, Leon won't be able to place 62 almonds on the tables.\n\nWait, let me check my math again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 27 times 7: 27*7. 20*7=140, 7*7=49, 140+49=189. Correct. Then 251-189. Let's do it another way. 189 + 60 = 249. Then 249 + 2 = 251. So, 60 + 2 = 62. Yep, that checks out. So the answer should be 62. \n\nI think that's it. He can place 189 almonds, so 251 - 189 = 62 almonds left. So, 62 is the number that can't be placed on the tables. I don't see any errors in the calculations. Seems straightforward. Maybe another way to think about it: 27*7=189, 251-189=62. Yep, that's correct. So the answer is 62.\n</think>\n\nLeon can place a maximum of \\(27 \\text{ almonds/table} \\times 7 \\text{ tables} = 189 \\text{ almonds}\\). Subtracting this from the total number of almonds gives \\(251 - 189 = 62\\). \n\nThe number of almonds he won't be able to place on the table is \\(\\boxed{62}\\).", "model_answer": 62.0, "ground_truth": 62.0, "is_correct": true}
