{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 662, "question": "Rania is an avid gardener. Two days ago, she received 26 new potted plants from her favorite plant nursery. She already has 10 potted plants on each of the 51 window ledges of her large country home. Feeling generous, she has decided that she will give 3 potted plants from each ledge to friends and family tomorrow. How many potted plants will Rania remain with?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Rania is an avid gardener. Two days ago, she received 26 new potted plants from her favorite plant nursery. She already has 10 potted plants on each of the 51 window ledges of her large country home. Feeling generous, she has decided that she will give 3 potted plants from each ledge to friends and family tomorrow. How many potted plants will Rania remain with?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Rania got 26 new potted plants from her nursery. She already has 10 plants on each of her 51 window ledges. Then she's going to give away 3 plants from each ledge tomorrow. The question is how many plants she'll have left.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out how many plants she has in total right now. She has 10 plants on each of the 51 ledges. So that's 10 multiplied by 51. Let me calculate that. 10 times 50 is 500, and then 10 more is 510. So she has 510 plants in total.\n\nThen, she's going to give away 3 plants from each ledge. Since there are 51 ledges, she's giving away 3 plants times 51 ledges. Let me do that multiplication. 3 times 50 is 150, and 3 times 1 is 3, so 150 plus 3 is 153. So she's giving away 153 plants in total.\n\nNow, to find out how many she has left, I subtract the number she gave away from the total she had. That's 510 minus 153. Let me do that subtraction. 510 minus 100 is 410, minus 50 is 360, minus 3 is 357. Wait, that seems a bit low. Let me check again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe break down the subtraction differently. 510 minus 150 is 360, and then minus 3 more is 357. Yeah, that's correct. So 510 - 153 = 357.\n\nWait, but let me verify once more to be sure. 153 plus 357 should equal 510. Let's add them. 150 + 350 is 500, and 5 + 7 is 12. So 500 + 12 is 512. Hmm, that's not right. Wait, that can't be. So maybe my subtraction was wrong.\n\nWait, hold on. If 153 + 357, let's do it step by step. 153 + 300 is 453, then +57 is 510. Oh, right. Because 357 + 100 is 457, then +50 is 507, +3 is 510. So 153 + 357 is indeed 510. So that checks out. So the answer is 357.\n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the initial calculations. Total plants: 10 per ledge times 51 ledges. 10*51=510. Correct. Then giving away 3 per ledge: 3*51=153. Then subtracting 153 from 510. 510-153. Let's do 510 - 150 = 360, then subtract 3 more: 360 - 3 = 357. Yes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way to think about it. If she gives away 3 plants from each of 51 ledges, that's 3*51=153 plants. So she started with 510 and gives away 153, so 510-153=357. Yep, that's the same result.\n\nSo I think that's the answer. 357 potted plants remaining.\n\n**Final Answer**\nRania will remain with \\boxed{357} potted plants.\n</think>\n\nRania received 26 new potted plants from her nursery. She already has 10 potted plants on each of her 51 window ledges. To find the total number of plants she has, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n10 \\text{ plants/ledge} \\times 51 \\text{ ledges} = 510 \\text{ plants}\n\\]\n\nShe plans to give away 3 potted plants from each ledge. The total number of plants she gives away is:\n\n\\[\n3 \\text{ plants/ledge} \\times 51 \\text{ ledges} = 153 \\text{ plants}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the number of plants she gives away from the total number of plants she has:\n\n\\[\n510 \\text{ plants} - 153 \\text{ plants} = 357 \\text{ plants}\n\\]\n\nThus, Rania will remain with \\boxed{357} potted plants.", "model_answer": 357.0, "ground_truth": 383.0, "is_correct": false}
