y. Therefore, the answer is 66 minutes. That seems correct.\n\nBut let me think again. Maybe the problem is that the cinema is part of the library journey? Like, maybe the cinema is a part of the path to the library? But the problem states: \"It takes them 24 minutes to get to the corner where the bank is. It takes them another 42 minutes to get to the cinema.\" So the journey is: start -> bank corner (24 min) -> cinema (42 min) -> library. Therefore, total time from start to library is 24 + 42 = 66 minutes. Therefore, the remaining time is 132 - 66 = 66 minutes. So they have 66 minutes left to reach the library. Therefore, the answer is 66 minutes. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the total time allocated is 132 minutes for the entire journey, which includes going to the bank, then to the cinema, then to the library. So the total time from start to library is 132 minutes. But according to the problem, the time from start to bank is 24 minutes, then from bank to cinema is 42 minutes, so total time to cinema is 66 minutes. Therefore, the remaining time to reach the library is 132 - 66 = 66 minutes. So yes, 66 minutes. Therefore, the answer is 66 minutes. So I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThey have \\boxed{66} minutes left to reach the library without being late.\n</think>\n\nLeon and Liam have a total of 132 minutes allocated to walk to the library together. They first take 24 minutes to reach the corner where the bank is. From there, it takes them another 42 minutes to reach the cinema. \n\nTo find out how much longer they have to reach the library, we calculate the total time they have already spent:\n\\[\n24 \\text{ minutes (to the bank)} + 42 \\text{ minutes (to the cinema)} = 66 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting this from the total allocated time gives the remaining time to reach the library:\n\\[\n132 \\text{ minutes (total)} - 66 \\text{ minutes (already spent)} = 66 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThus, they