, they pass the cinema in 19 minutes and the park in another 13 minutes. So the time from start to cinema is 19 minutes, then from cinema to park is 13 minutes. So total time spent traveling is 19 + 13 = 32 minutes. Therefore, remaining time is 91 - 32 = 59 minutes. So they have 59 minutes left to reach the bank. That seems straightforward.\n\nBut let me check if there's any trick here. Sometimes problems might involve rates or distances, but since all the time intervals are given in minutes and the total time is also in minutes, it's likely a straightforward time subtraction problem. There's no mention of distances or speeds, so we don't need to consider speed or distance here. It's just about total time allocated versus time already spent.\n\nSo, to recap: Total time available = 91 minutes. Time spent reaching cinema = 19 minutes. Time spent from cinema to park = 13 minutes. Total time spent so far = 19 + 13 = 32 minutes. Time remaining = 91 - 32 = 59 minutes. Therefore, they have 59 minutes left to reach the bank without being late.\n\nI think that's it. The key is to add up the time spent on the two segments (cinema and park) and subtract that from the total time to find the remaining time. There's no mention of any other stops or changes in direction, so it's a simple subtraction problem. Yeah, that makes sense. I don't see any hidden steps or complexities here. The answer should be 59 minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThey have \\boxed{59} minutes left to reach the bank without being late.\n</think>\n\nAisha and Viktor have a total of 91 minutes to walk to the bank. They spend 19 minutes to reach the cinema corner and then another 13 minutes to reach the park. \n\nFirst, we calculate the total time they have already spent:\n\\[\n19 \\text{ minutes (cinema)} + 13 \\text{ minutes (park)} = 32 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the time they have already spent from the total time allocated to find the remaining time they have to reach the bank:\n\\[\n91 \\tex