, the answer should be 8 minutes. Let me just check if I did that right.\n\nWait, let me verify. Total time: 78 minutes. Time to cinema: 29 minutes. Time from cinema to library: 41 minutes. So total time from start to library is 29 + 41 = 70 minutes. Therefore, time left to bank is 78 - 70 = 8 minutes. Yeah, that seems straightforward. But let me make sure there's no trick here. The problem says \"how much longer do they have to get to bank without being late?\" So they have already used up 70 minutes, so they have 8 minutes left. So the answer is 8 minutes. I think that's correct. Let me just rephrase the problem in my own words to check understanding.\n\nSuppose they start walking at time 0. They take 29 minutes to reach the cinema corner. Then, from there, another 41 minutes to reach the library. So by the time they reach the library, they have used up 29 + 41 = 70 minutes. They were supposed to have 78 minutes total. So the time left is 78 - 70 = 8 minutes. Therefore, they have 8 minutes left to reach the bank. So the answer is 8 minutes. Yeah, that seems right. I don't see any other factors here. The problem doesn't mention any other stops or changes in direction. It's just cumulative time. So subtract the time spent from the total time to get the remaining time. Yep, 8 minutes. I think that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThey have \\boxed{8} minutes left to reach the bank without being late.\n</think>\n\nIsabella and Santiago have a total of 78 minutes to walk to the bank together. They spend 29 minutes to reach the cinema corner and another 41 minutes to reach the library. \n\nFirst, we calculate the total time spent traveling to the library:\n\\[\n29 \\text{ minutes} + 41 \\text{ minutes} = 70 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the time already spent from the total time they have:\n\\[\n78 \\text{ minutes} - 70 \\text{ minutes} = 8 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, they have \\boxed{8} minutes left to reach the bank without being late.", "model