%. Then Esteban's 150 out of 460. 150 ÷ 460. Divide numerator and denominator by 10: 15 ÷ 46 ≈ 0.326086... So approximately 32.61%. Adding those two percentages: 21.57% + 32.61% ≈ 54.18%. Wait, that's not 25%. Wait, that can't be. Wait, hold on, that's conflicting with the previous result. Wait, what's going on here?\n\nWait, no, hold on. Wait, 220 out of 1020 is 21.57%, and 150 out of 460 is 32.61%, so adding them gives 54.18%, but according to the previous calculation, it's 25%. That's a discrepancy. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check again. Wait, 220 + 150 = 370. 1020 + 460 = 1480. 370 / 1480 = 0.25. So 25%. But when I calculated each separately as percentages and added them, I got 21.57% + 32.61% = 54.18%. That's way off. So, where is the mistake here?\n\nAh! Wait a second. Because when you take the percentage of each, you're not just adding the percentages, you're adding the actual numbers. But percentages are relative to their own totals. So, for example, 220 out of 1020 is 21.57%, but that's relative to Theo's total. Similarly, 150 out of 460 is 32.61%, relative to Esteban's total. But when you want the percentage of all the puppies, you need to take the total spotted over total puppies, which is 370 over 1480. So, the mistake was in adding the percentages instead of adding the actual numbers. So, the correct way is to add the actual numbers (370) and divide by the total (1480). So, 370/1480=0.25=25%. So, the initial calculation was correct, and the second method was wrong because I was adding percentages instead of actual numbers. That's a common mistake. So, important to remember to add the actual counts, not the percentages.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 25%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe percentage of all the puppies that have spots is \\boxed{25\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find", "model_answer": 25.0, "ground_truth": 25.0, "is_correct": true}
