en on Saturday he sold W_Su - 17. But the problem says \"17 fewer boxes of watermelon pie, than on Sunday.\" So yes, that would mean W_S = W_Su - 17.\n\nBut then on Sunday, he sold 29 more watermelon pies than on Saturday, so W_Su = W_S + 29. Therefore, substituting W_S = W_Su - 17 into W_Su = W_S + 29:\n\nW_Su = (W_Su - 17) + 29 => W_Su = W_Su + 12 => 0 = 12. Wait, that can't be. That's a contradiction. So that suggests that my initial interpretation is wrong.\n\nWait, so if on Saturday he sold 27 boxes of brownie, and 17 fewer watermelon pies than on Sunday, and on Sunday he sold 11 more brownies than on Saturday and 29 more watermelon pies than on Saturday. Then, if B_S = 27, then B_Su = 27 + 11 = 38. Then W_S = W_Su - 17, and W_Su = W_S + 29. Therefore, substituting:\n\nW_S = (W_S + 29) - 17 => W_S = W_S + 12 => 0 = 12. Which is impossible. Therefore, this suggests that the initial assumption that B_S = 27 is incorrect.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"On Saturday, he sold 27 boxes of brownie and 17 fewer boxes of watermelon pie, than on Sunday.\" So maybe the 27 is the number of brownie boxes sold on Saturday, and the watermelon pie is 17 fewer. So B_S = 27, W_S = W_Su - 17. Then on Sunday, he sold B_Su = B_S + 11 = 38, and W_Su = W_S + 29. But since W_S = W_Su - 17, substituting into W_Su =", "model_answer": 17.0, "ground_truth": 1586.0, "is_correct": false}
