{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 861, "question": "Zahra walks 30 miles every day on her favorite walking trail, which includes 15 miles of walking on the beach and 15 miles of walking on the sidewalk. On the sidewalk, Zahra walks at two times the rate of speed that she does on the beach. If 60 minutes of her walk is spent on the beach, how long does it take for her to complete the entire 30-mile walk, in minutes?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Zahra walks 30 miles every day on her favorite walking trail, which includes 15 miles of walking on the beach and 15 miles of walking on the sidewalk. On the sidewalk, Zahra walks at two times the rate of speed that she does on the beach. If 60 minutes of her walk is spent on the beach, how long does it take for her to complete the entire 30-mile walk, in minutes?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Zahra walks 30 miles every day, right? The trail has 15 miles on the beach and 15 miles on the sidewalk. On the sidewalk, she walks twice as fast as she does on the beach. And the problem says that 60 minutes of her walk is spent on the beach. They want to know how long it takes her to complete the entire 30-mile walk in minutes. Hmm, okay.\n\nFirst, let me break this down. The total walk is 30 miles, split into two parts: 15 miles on the beach and 15 miles on the sidewalk. The time she spends on the beach is given as 60 minutes. So, the time on the sidewalk must be unknown, and we need to find the total time for the entire walk.\n\nSince speed is distance divided by time, maybe I can use that formula for both parts of the walk. Let me denote her walking speed on the beach as 'v' miles per minute. Then, her speed on the sidewalk would be '2v' miles per minute because she walks twice as fast there.\n\nWait, the problem says 60 minutes of her walk is spent on the beach. So, the time on the beach is 60 minutes. Therefore, the time on the sidewalk would be