uation, I can solve for 'v'.\n\nLet me write that down:\n\n11 / v = 66\n\nTo solve for 'v', multiply both sides by 'v':\n\n11 = 66v\n\nThen divide both sides by 66:\n\nv = 11 / 66\n\nSimplify that:\n\nv = 1/6 miles per minute\n\nWait, that seems slow. Let me check. If her speed on the beach is 1/6 miles per minute, then 11 miles divided by 1/6 miles per minute is 11 * 6 = 66 minutes. Yeah, that checks out. So her speed on the beach is 1/6 mph? Wait, no, the units here are miles per minute. So 1/6 miles per minute is equivalent to 1/6 * 60 minutes per hour, which is 10 miles per hour. That seems reasonable for walking speed.\n\nOkay, so her speed on the beach is 10 mph, and on the sidewalk, it's five times that, so 50 mph. Wait, 5 times 10 is 50. But 50 mph is extremely fast for walking. That's like a running speed. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, hold on. The problem says she walks on the beach and on the sidewalk. So maybe the units are in miles per minute, but converting to mph might be necessary. Let me check again.\n\nIf her speed on the beach is 1/6 miles per minute, then in one hour (60 minutes), she walks 1/6 * 60 = 10 miles. So 10 mph. That's a normal walking speed. 50 mph on the sidewalk is way too fast. That's like a sprint. Maybe the problem is using miles per minute for both? But that would be inconsistent. Wait, maybe the problem is using different units for speed. Let me re-examine the problem statement.\n\nThe problem says: \"Xena walks 21 miles every day on her favorite walking trail, which includes 11 miles of walking on the beach and 10 miles of walking on the sidewalk. On the sidewalk, Xena walks at five times the rate of speed that she does on the beach.\"\n\nSo, the problem mentions \"rate of speed\" on the sidewalk is five times that on the beach. So, the rate is five times. So, if her speed on the beach is 's' miles per minute, then her speed on the sidewalk is '5s' miles per minute. So, the units here are miles per minute. So, 5 ti